WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.576 --> 00:14.316 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference April 29,

00:15.026 --> 00:18.776 align:middle
2026 CHAIR POWELL.

00:18.926 --> 00:19.906 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:19.906 --> 00:21.946 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:21.946 --> 00:25.066 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:25.066 --> 00:28.326 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:28.326 --> 00:32.026 align:middle
The U.S. economy has been
expanding at a solid pace.

00:32.336 --> 00:33.926 align:middle
While job gains have
remained low,

00:34.366 --> 00:37.286 align:middle
the unemployment rate has been
little changed in recent months.

00:38.186 --> 00:40.056 align:middle
Inflation has moved
up and is elevated,

00:40.106 --> 00:41.986 align:middle
in part reflecting
the recent increase

00:42.106 --> 00:43.696 align:middle
in global energy prices.

00:44.906 --> 00:48.626 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided to
leave our policy rate unchanged.

00:49.596 --> 00:52.556 align:middle
We see the current stance of
monetary policy as appropriate

00:52.556 --> 00:54.996 align:middle
to promote progress toward
our maximum-employment

00:55.396 --> 00:57.196 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation goals.

00:58.906 --> 01:01.506 align:middle
Developments in the Middle East
are contributing to a high level

01:01.506 --> 01:03.436 align:middle
of uncertainty about
the economic outlook,

01:04.126 --> 01:05.806 align:middle
and we will remain
attentive to risks

01:05.806 --> 01:07.406 align:middle
to both sides of
our dual mandate.

01:08.406 --> 01:10.246 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

01:10.246 --> 01:12.856 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:14.676 --> 01:15.936 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest

01:15.936 --> 01:19.026 align:middle
that economic activity has
been expanding at a solid pace.

01:19.836 --> 01:21.466 align:middle
Consumer spending
has been resilient,

01:22.186 --> 01:24.106 align:middle
and business fixed
investment has continued

01:24.106 --> 01:26.026 align:middle
to expand at a brisk pace.

01:26.656 --> 01:28.166 align:middle
In contrast, activity

01:28.166 --> 01:30.236 align:middle
in the housing sector
has remained weak.

01:31.086 --> 01:32.676 align:middle
In the labor market,

01:32.676 --> 01:35.436 align:middle
the unemployment rate
was 4.3 percent in March

01:35.436 --> 01:37.386 align:middle
and has changed little
in recent months.

01:38.316 --> 01:39.756 align:middle
Job gains have remained low.

01:41.036 --> 01:43.716 align:middle
A good part of the slowing
in the pace of the job growth

01:43.776 --> 01:46.086 align:middle
over the past year reflects
a decline in the growth

01:46.196 --> 01:49.046 align:middle
of the labor force due
to lower immigration

01:49.046 --> 01:50.446 align:middle
and labor force participation,

01:51.156 --> 01:53.566 align:middle
though labor demand has
clearly softened as well.

01:54.846 --> 01:58.106 align:middle
Other indicators, including
job openings, layoffs, hiring,

01:58.106 --> 01:59.136 align:middle
and nominal wage growth,

01:59.556 --> 02:02.076 align:middle
generally show little
change in recent months.

02:04.206 --> 02:07.446 align:middle
Inflation has moved up recently
and is elevated relative

02:07.446 --> 02:09.326 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:10.466 --> 02:12.616 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

02:12.746 --> 02:13.936 align:middle
and other data indicate

02:13.936 --> 02:16.816 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 3.5 percent

02:17.216 --> 02:18.766 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in March,

02:19.546 --> 02:22.546 align:middle
boosted by the significant
rise in global oil prices

02:22.546 --> 02:24.916 align:middle
that has resulted from the
conflict in the Middle East.

02:26.626 --> 02:28.916 align:middle
Excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:29.136 --> 02:31.826 align:middle
core PCE prices rose 3.2 percent

02:31.826 --> 02:33.526 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in March.

02:34.656 --> 02:37.166 align:middle
This relatively high rate
largely reflects the effects

02:37.166 --> 02:39.106 align:middle
of tariffs on prices
in the goods sector.

02:40.206 --> 02:43.246 align:middle
Near-term measures of inflation
expectations have risen this

02:43.246 --> 02:46.936 align:middle
year, likely because of the
substantial rise in oil prices.

02:48.206 --> 02:51.096 align:middle
Most measures of longer-term
expectations remain consistent

02:51.096 --> 02:52.806 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

02:54.726 --> 02:57.876 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

02:57.876 --> 03:00.276 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:00.276 --> 03:01.236 align:middle
for the American people.

03:02.346 --> 03:03.926 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:03.926 --> 03:06.196 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the federal funds rate

03:06.196 --> 03:08.486 align:middle
at 3½ to 3  percent.

03:09.836 --> 03:12.036 align:middle
The economic outlook
remains highly uncertain,

03:12.196 --> 03:14.436 align:middle
and the conflict in the
Middle East has added

03:14.436 --> 03:15.366 align:middle
to this uncertainty.

03:16.876 --> 03:19.146 align:middle
In the near term, higher
energy prices will push

03:19.146 --> 03:20.436 align:middle
up overall inflation.

03:21.366 --> 03:24.106 align:middle
Beyond that, the scope and
duration of potential effects

03:24.106 --> 03:27.436 align:middle
on the economy remain unclear,
as does the future course

03:27.436 --> 03:28.586 align:middle
of the conflict itself.

03:29.766 --> 03:31.526 align:middle
We will continue to
monitor the risks

03:31.526 --> 03:33.186 align:middle
to both sides of
our dual mandate.

03:34.096 --> 03:36.556 align:middle
We are well positioned to
determine the extent and timing

03:36.556 --> 03:39.216 align:middle
of additional adjustments
to our policy rate based

03:39.216 --> 03:41.596 align:middle
on the incoming data,
the evolving outlook,

03:41.806 --> 03:42.966 align:middle
and the balance of risks.

03:44.206 --> 03:46.476 align:middle
Monetary policy is not
on a preset course,

03:47.056 --> 03:49.936 align:middle
and we will make our decisions
on a meeting-by-meeting basis.

03:52.246 --> 03:54.866 align:middle
This is my last press
conference as Chair,

03:54.866 --> 03:56.826 align:middle
and I will close
with a few thoughts.

03:57.436 --> 04:00.736 align:middle
First, I want to congratulate
Kevin Warsh on his advancement

04:00.736 --> 04:02.486 align:middle
out of the Senate Banking
Committee this morning.

04:03.016 --> 04:05.896 align:middle
This is an important step
forward, and I wish him well

04:05.896 --> 04:07.676 align:middle
as that process continues.

04:09.676 --> 04:12.496 align:middle
The Federal Reserve exists
for one fundamental purpose:

04:12.676 --> 04:14.216 align:middle
to foster the economic
conditions

04:14.216 --> 04:15.986 align:middle
in which American families

04:15.986 --> 04:19.936 align:middle
and businesses can thrive-stable
prices, a strong job market,

04:20.346 --> 04:22.286 align:middle
and a financial system
they can depend on.

04:23.446 --> 04:25.946 align:middle
Every decision we make-whether
about interest rates,

04:25.946 --> 04:29.426 align:middle
or regulatory and supervisory
matters, or other issues-is made

04:29.426 --> 04:30.876 align:middle
in service of that purpose.

04:32.306 --> 04:34.556 align:middle
Our decisions reflect the
collective judgment of the Board

04:34.556 --> 04:37.176 align:middle
of Governors and the Federal
Open Market Committee-colleagues

04:37.176 --> 04:40.206 align:middle
who demonstrate analytical
rigor, principled judgment,

04:40.276 --> 04:42.686 align:middle
and a genuine commitment
to the public interest.

04:43.486 --> 04:44.276 align:middle
Our collaborative

04:44.416 --> 04:47.076 align:middle
and deliberative process
has long reflected a shared

04:47.076 --> 04:49.146 align:middle
commitment to finding
common ground

04:49.546 --> 04:50.646 align:middle
in service to our mission.

04:52.226 --> 04:54.536 align:middle
This institution is
resilient, capable,

04:54.536 --> 04:57.176 align:middle
and staffed by professionals
of extraordinary talent

04:57.286 --> 04:58.636 align:middle
and exceptional dedication.

04:59.476 --> 05:01.496 align:middle
It has been a privilege
to serve alongside

05:01.586 --> 05:04.966 align:middle
so many great public servants
at the Board of Governors

05:04.966 --> 05:06.486 align:middle
and around the Federal
Reserve System.

05:08.786 --> 05:10.306 align:middle
The Fed's work is
only as effective

05:10.306 --> 05:13.066 align:middle
as the public's understanding
of it, and you, the press,

05:13.406 --> 05:15.786 align:middle
are essential to keeping
the public informed

05:15.846 --> 05:17.106 align:middle
about what we do and why.

05:17.826 --> 05:20.926 align:middle
The people we serve benefit
from your careful reporting.

05:23.266 --> 05:26.286 align:middle
I welcomed the announcement
last Friday by the U.S. Attorney

05:26.286 --> 05:27.666 align:middle
for the District of Columbia

05:28.056 --> 05:29.926 align:middle
that she had closed the
criminal investigation.

05:30.656 --> 05:33.266 align:middle
She also noted, however,
that she would not hesitate

05:33.266 --> 05:34.616 align:middle
to restart the investigation.

05:35.746 --> 05:38.836 align:middle
Over the weekend, the Department
of Justice provided assurances

05:39.176 --> 05:41.676 align:middle
that they will not reopen the
investigation unless there is a

05:41.706 --> 05:44.696 align:middle
criminal referral from the
Fed's Inspector General.

05:45.166 --> 05:47.036 align:middle
And, absent such a referral,

05:47.516 --> 05:49.406 align:middle
if they do appeal the
recent court decision,

05:49.576 --> 05:51.666 align:middle
they would not seek
as part of that appeal

05:52.046 --> 05:55.496 align:middle
to restart the investigation
or send new subpoenas.

05:56.816 --> 05:59.426 align:middle
I have said that I will
not leave the Board

05:59.426 --> 06:02.056 align:middle
until this investigation
is well and truly over,

06:02.606 --> 06:05.296 align:middle
with transparency and
finality, and I stand by that.

06:06.056 --> 06:07.806 align:middle
I'm encouraged by
recent developments,

06:07.916 --> 06:11.736 align:middle
and I'm watching the remaining
steps in this process carefully.

06:13.406 --> 06:15.116 align:middle
My decisions on these
matters will continue

06:15.116 --> 06:17.926 align:middle
to be guided entirely by what I
believe is in the best interest

06:17.926 --> 06:19.776 align:middle
of the institution and
the people we serve.

06:21.226 --> 06:24.576 align:middle
After my term as Chair ends on
May 15, I will continue to serve

06:24.576 --> 06:27.816 align:middle
as a Governor for a period
of time to be determined.

06:28.906 --> 06:31.076 align:middle
I plan to keep a low
profile as a Governor.

06:31.726 --> 06:34.836 align:middle
There is only ever one Chair
of the Federal Reserve Board.

06:35.436 --> 06:37.606 align:middle
When Kevin Warsh is
confirmed and sworn in,

06:37.716 --> 06:38.606 align:middle
he will be that Chair.

06:39.516 --> 06:40.906 align:middle
Once sworn in as Board Chair,

06:40.956 --> 06:44.286 align:middle
his new colleagues will elect
him to chair the FOMC as well.

06:45.106 --> 06:48.406 align:middle
As I regularly point
out from this podium,

06:48.656 --> 06:52.326 align:middle
our success in delivering on our
goals matters for all Americans.

06:52.566 --> 06:55.806 align:middle
I am confident that the Fed
will continue to do its work

06:55.806 --> 06:58.566 align:middle
with objectivity, integrity,
and a deep commitment

06:58.566 --> 07:00.116 align:middle
to serve the American people.

07:01.176 --> 07:03.036 align:middle
Thank you, and I look
forward to your questions.

07:03.806 --> 07:05.626 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:08.076 --> 07:11.106 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

07:11.106 --> 07:12.026 align:middle
I appreciate the kind words

07:12.026 --> 07:13.896 align:middle
about the press-often
doesn't come from the podium

07:13.896 --> 07:15.336 align:middle
in different places,
but appreciate that.

07:16.136 --> 07:19.616 align:middle
Can you talk about what
has gone into your decision

07:19.616 --> 07:20.476 align:middle
to remain on the Board?

07:20.476 --> 07:22.136 align:middle
What kind of criteria
are you weighing,

07:22.136 --> 07:23.816 align:middle
and how long might you stay?

07:23.816 --> 07:24.186 align:middle
Thank you.

07:24.186 --> 07:24.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:25.046 --> 07:29.476 align:middle
Sure. So, you know, my concern
is really about the series

07:29.476 --> 07:32.626 align:middle
of legal attacks on the Fed
which threaten our ability

07:32.626 --> 07:33.826 align:middle
to conduct monetary policy

07:33.826 --> 07:35.576 align:middle
without considering
political factors.

07:36.286 --> 07:39.196 align:middle
And I want to note here this
has nothing whatever to do

07:39.196 --> 07:41.416 align:middle
with verbal criticism
by elected officials.

07:41.946 --> 07:45.826 align:middle
I've never suggested that such
verbal criticism is a problem,

07:45.826 --> 07:46.976 align:middle
and neither has anyone
else here.

07:47.646 --> 07:50.746 align:middle
But these legal actions by the
Administration are unprecedented

07:50.746 --> 07:54.436 align:middle
in our 113-year history, and
there are ongoing threats

07:54.796 --> 07:56.446 align:middle
of additional such actions.

07:56.446 --> 07:59.636 align:middle
I worry that these attacks
are battering the institution

07:59.946 --> 08:01.826 align:middle
and putting at risk the
thing that really matters

08:01.826 --> 08:03.696 align:middle
to the public-which
is the ability

08:03.696 --> 08:05.896 align:middle
to conduct monetary
policy without taking

08:05.896 --> 08:07.606 align:middle
into consideration
political factors.

08:08.206 --> 08:10.626 align:middle
It is so important for our
economy, for the people

08:10.626 --> 08:13.166 align:middle
that we serve, that they
can depend over time

08:13.166 --> 08:15.106 align:middle
on a central bank that
operates that way,

08:15.406 --> 08:17.276 align:middle
free of political influence.

08:17.846 --> 08:19.756 align:middle
It's part of the
absolute foundation

08:19.906 --> 08:21.636 align:middle
of this amazing economy
that we have.

08:21.636 --> 08:25.236 align:middle
It's just one of the many
reasons why the U.S. economy is

08:25.316 --> 08:26.216 align:middle
the envy of the world.

08:26.796 --> 08:29.686 align:middle
That piece of institutional
architecture separates

08:29.686 --> 08:31.906 align:middle
successful countries from
unsuccessful countries.

08:32.506 --> 08:34.996 align:middle
It is extremely important-not
for the people who work

08:34.996 --> 08:37.466 align:middle
at the Fed at any given
time, but for the people

08:37.466 --> 08:39.556 align:middle
that we serve-that
the Fed remain able

08:39.556 --> 08:42.166 align:middle
to conduct monetary policy in
a way that doesn't get pulled

08:42.166 --> 08:43.936 align:middle
into politics, trying to help

08:43.936 --> 08:46.706 align:middle
or hurt any particular
politician or political party.

08:47.066 --> 08:48.666 align:middle
It's critical for the
people that we serve.

08:49.326 --> 08:51.866 align:middle
In terms of when I would
leave, I will leave

08:51.966 --> 08:53.486 align:middle
when I think it's
appropriate to do so.

08:53.486 --> 08:55.166 align:middle
Was that all your questions?

08:55.166 --> 08:55.516 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

08:55.516 --> 08:57.756 align:middle
Well, I just have a follow-up,
which is, what would you say

08:57.756 --> 09:00.556 align:middle
to the criticism that by
remaining on the Board,

09:00.556 --> 09:02.156 align:middle
you're actually taking
a political act

09:02.156 --> 09:05.966 align:middle
and denying President Trump the
majority of the Board, which,

09:05.966 --> 09:07.586 align:middle
as President, he would
have if you left?

09:08.106 --> 09:08.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:08.676 --> 09:09.726 align:middle
I don't see that at all.

09:09.726 --> 09:12.866 align:middle
As I mentioned, you know,
I'm literally staying

09:12.866 --> 09:14.656 align:middle
because of the actions
that have been taken.

09:14.866 --> 09:17.396 align:middle
I had long planned
to be retiring.

09:17.906 --> 09:20.346 align:middle
And, you know, the things
that have happened, really,

09:20.346 --> 09:23.326 align:middle
in the last three months have,
I think, left me no choice

09:23.366 --> 09:27.206 align:middle
but to stay until I see them
through at least that long.

09:27.506 --> 09:31.936 align:middle
You know, in addition, I don't
see how this will interfere.

09:31.936 --> 09:33.766 align:middle
My intention is not
to interfere.

09:33.866 --> 09:36.236 align:middle
You know, I was a Governor
for almost six years,

09:36.706 --> 09:39.526 align:middle
and the tradition is, at
the Fed, that Governors

09:39.526 --> 09:42.786 align:middle
who understand how difficult
the role of Chair is-and

09:42.786 --> 09:46.556 align:middle
as a soon-to-be former Chair,
I do understand how hard it is

09:46.556 --> 09:50.366 align:middle
to get consensus with 19
strong-minded people-you work

09:50.366 --> 09:57.326 align:middle
with the Chair, you try to be
heard, but also collaborate

09:57.326 --> 09:59.706 align:middle
with the Chair and try to
support the Chair when you can.

09:59.706 --> 10:00.526 align:middle
When you can't, you can't.

10:00.526 --> 10:03.376 align:middle
And I think that's the attitude
that people generally take.

10:03.376 --> 10:04.526 align:middle
And that's the attitude
that I'll take.

10:04.646 --> 10:07.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:07.476 --> 10:12.236 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:12.236 --> 10:14.406 align:middle
Chair Powell, if I could ask
about the inflation outlook.

10:15.386 --> 10:19.126 align:middle
In March, you described
the standard practice

10:19.126 --> 10:21.676 align:middle
of "looking through"
energy shocks as conditional

10:21.866 --> 10:24.386 align:middle
on inflation expectations
staying anchored.

10:25.356 --> 10:27.896 align:middle
Since that meeting, there
has been very little progress

10:27.896 --> 10:30.656 align:middle
reopening key energy
trade corridors.

10:31.056 --> 10:34.376 align:middle
Can you help us understand
how the inflation outlook has

10:34.376 --> 10:36.306 align:middle
changed in the intermeeting
period,

10:36.406 --> 10:39.286 align:middle
beginning with the prospects for
tariff pass-through resolving

10:39.286 --> 10:41.366 align:middle
on the timeline that you
had outlined in March,

10:41.886 --> 10:45.176 align:middle
before getting to the energy
shock that is now on top?

10:45.876 --> 10:46.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:47.156 --> 10:49.116 align:middle
So, you know, I would
look at it this way.

10:49.916 --> 10:55.496 align:middle
For a long time, we've been
working on the hypothesis,

10:55.496 --> 10:58.696 align:middle
really, that tariff,
tariffs would lead

10:58.696 --> 11:01.386 align:middle
to a one-time price increase
and that that would go away

11:01.386 --> 11:02.646 align:middle
over time-in other words,

11:02.646 --> 11:03.966 align:middle
that there would be
no further change,

11:03.966 --> 11:06.886 align:middle
so measured inflation wouldn't
reflect that higher level,

11:07.356 --> 11:08.256 align:middle
going up more and more.

11:09.066 --> 11:10.846 align:middle
And it's time for
that to happen.

11:10.926 --> 11:12.996 align:middle
You know, we really do
expect that to be happening

11:13.616 --> 11:14.606 align:middle
in the next two quarters.

11:14.606 --> 11:16.876 align:middle
So we'll be watching
very carefully to see

11:16.876 --> 11:18.866 align:middle
that what we've thought
all along would happen.

11:18.866 --> 11:21.026 align:middle
That's the kind of critical
part of the forecast.

11:21.026 --> 11:22.386 align:middle
We need to really see that.

11:23.146 --> 11:25.736 align:middle
With, with energy,
it's so hard to say.

11:25.736 --> 11:28.236 align:middle
I mentioned, you
know, in, you know,

11:28.236 --> 11:29.196 align:middle
sort of the textbook [analysis],

11:29.686 --> 11:32.706 align:middle
you would "look through" an
oil shock because they tend

11:32.706 --> 11:34.556 align:middle
to be short lived and
they tend to revert.

11:35.006 --> 11:38.016 align:middle
And monetary policy works with
long and variable lags, so,

11:38.486 --> 11:41.026 align:middle
you know, you wouldn't
necessarily react right away.

11:41.026 --> 11:42.856 align:middle
I think that is all
the more true,

11:42.996 --> 11:45.776 align:middle
given that we're several years
above 2 percent inflation,

11:46.066 --> 11:48.876 align:middle
and that we're already "looking
through" the tariff shock.

11:49.216 --> 11:51.416 align:middle
So I think we're going to
be very cautious about that.

11:51.416 --> 11:53.986 align:middle
It's-but the, you know,
the question about looking

11:53.986 --> 11:56.346 align:middle
through energy really is not
in front of us right now.

11:56.346 --> 11:57.596 align:middle
It hasn't even peaked yet.

11:58.046 --> 12:00.916 align:middle
And I think we'd want to see the
back side of that and progress

12:01.366 --> 12:03.536 align:middle
on tariffs before we
even thought about,

12:04.216 --> 12:05.126 align:middle
about reducing rates.

12:05.276 --> 12:05.806 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:05.806 --> 12:06.926 align:middle
So if I could follow up:

12:06.986 --> 12:09.806 align:middle
The statement today preserves
language that has taken

12:09.806 --> 12:12.806 align:middle
on some meaning, as
it was socialized

12:12.806 --> 12:14.616 align:middle
when the Committee was
actively lowering rates.

12:14.976 --> 12:18.296 align:middle
Why is that easing
bias still ripe,

12:19.106 --> 12:22.916 align:middle
given how different the
inflation outlook is now

12:22.916 --> 12:24.486 align:middle
versus a meeting or two ago?

12:24.936 --> 12:28.316 align:middle
And what more would have to
happen for it to get evicted?

12:28.316 --> 12:29.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:29.216 --> 12:33.986 align:middle
So that was-as you will recall,
we had a discussion about that

12:34.076 --> 12:35.566 align:middle
at the last meeting,
and we talked about it

12:35.566 --> 12:37.576 align:middle
in the press conference
after the March meeting.

12:38.236 --> 12:39.056 align:middle
We had the same today.

12:39.056 --> 12:41.956 align:middle
We had quite a vigorous
discussion about that very issue

12:41.956 --> 12:43.956 align:middle
and the guidance and "is
it still appropriate"

12:43.956 --> 12:44.536 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

12:44.986 --> 12:47.686 align:middle
And I would say that the,
you know, number of people

12:47.686 --> 12:49.456 align:middle
on the Committee who
either could support

12:49.556 --> 12:53.776 align:middle
that language change changing
to a more neutral stance

12:53.776 --> 12:58.446 align:middle
so that the hike is as likely as
a cut-that number has increased

12:58.566 --> 12:59.716 align:middle
over the intermeeting period.

12:59.716 --> 13:00.916 align:middle
And it's easy to see why.

13:00.916 --> 13:03.846 align:middle
I mean, it's, it's a
good question, right?

13:03.886 --> 13:05.406 align:middle
You see inflation has moved

13:05.406 --> 13:08.486 align:middle
up over the interim a bit-core
inflation's 3.2 [percent]

13:08.486 --> 13:12.166 align:middle
now-moving albeit just a little
bit in the wrong direction.

13:12.626 --> 13:15.296 align:middle
And we know that there
will be, you know,

13:15.296 --> 13:18.656 align:middle
that there's headline
inflation coming out of the Gulf

13:18.806 --> 13:20.306 align:middle
and we don't know how
much that will be,

13:21.096 --> 13:22.496 align:middle
we just-we're going
to need to see.

13:22.496 --> 13:25.396 align:middle
So it makes all the sense in
the world that people would look

13:25.396 --> 13:27.926 align:middle
at that and we'd have a
vigorous discussion about that.

13:27.926 --> 13:30.506 align:middle
You saw that three people
dissented over the language.

13:30.836 --> 13:34.526 align:middle
I think all of those people
agreed with the rate decision.

13:35.006 --> 13:38.256 align:middle
So the majority of the Committee
did not want to do that.

13:38.256 --> 13:41.726 align:middle
And, and I was-I didn't think we
needed to do it at this meeting.

13:41.726 --> 13:42.886 align:middle
It really was just a question

13:42.886 --> 13:45.756 align:middle
of what's-why do we
need to do that now?

13:45.756 --> 13:47.196 align:middle
You know, we have
so much to learn,

13:47.196 --> 13:49.216 align:middle
there's so much uncertainty
about the path ahead,

13:49.696 --> 13:51.786 align:middle
there doesn't need to
be any rush to make

13:51.786 --> 13:54.616 align:middle
that decision now because,
you know, what happens

13:54.616 --> 13:57.086 align:middle
in the next 30, 60 days,
even by the next meeting,

13:57.426 --> 13:58.926 align:middle
could really change the picture

13:59.046 --> 14:00.606 align:middle
around that-around
that language.

14:00.606 --> 14:05.176 align:middle
So, you know, it was a-it's a
close-it's a much closer thing

14:05.176 --> 14:06.716 align:middle
on the Committee
than it was in March.

14:07.086 --> 14:08.426 align:middle
And, you know, that
makes all the sense

14:08.426 --> 14:09.296 align:middle
in the world, it seems to me.

14:09.296 --> 14:09.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:11.276 --> 14:14.046 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

14:14.076 --> 14:16.656 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

14:16.756 --> 14:21.386 align:middle
Just going back to this
issue of the easing bias:

14:22.076 --> 14:26.136 align:middle
We've now got oil approaching
$120 a barrel when it comes

14:26.136 --> 14:29.746 align:middle
to the benchmark Brent crude.

14:30.216 --> 14:33.596 align:middle
If it stays around those
levels six weeks from now,

14:33.596 --> 14:35.556 align:middle
what would be your
guess-best guess

14:35.556 --> 14:38.676 align:middle
as to whether the easing bias
will still be in the statement?

14:38.956 --> 14:39.316 align:middle
Thank you.

14:39.316 --> 14:40.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:40.486 --> 14:41.396 align:middle
I wouldn't want to guess.

14:41.396 --> 14:42.086 align:middle
You know-well, first of all,

14:42.086 --> 14:43.426 align:middle
we're going to have
new leadership,

14:43.426 --> 14:44.546 align:middle
in all likelihood, by then.

14:44.716 --> 14:47.026 align:middle
And new leadership is going
to have a very important role

14:47.026 --> 14:50.946 align:middle
to play in that, so I won't be
standing here at this podium

14:50.946 --> 14:52.046 align:middle
to answer your questions.

14:52.576 --> 14:53.676 align:middle
I don't know.

14:53.826 --> 14:55.966 align:middle
As I mentioned, that's
all I can really say is

14:55.966 --> 14:58.146 align:middle
that we had a great
discussion about that today.

14:58.146 --> 15:00.956 align:middle
It's a-you know, it's got-it's
gotten to be a better question

15:00.956 --> 15:01.846 align:middle
in the interim period.

15:02.196 --> 15:03.146 align:middle
We had the discussion.

15:03.146 --> 15:06.686 align:middle
A majority are still on the
page of not feeling the need

15:06.686 --> 15:07.856 align:middle
to move to that level.

15:07.856 --> 15:09.116 align:middle
And I-that's where I am.

15:09.656 --> 15:10.986 align:middle
I get it, though.

15:11.056 --> 15:13.156 align:middle
You know, at a certain
point, you would move.

15:13.156 --> 15:15.186 align:middle
And that, that conceivably
could come

15:15.186 --> 15:16.166 align:middle
as soon as the next meeting.

15:16.166 --> 15:17.246 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

15:17.246 --> 15:17.466 align:middle
Thank you.

15:17.466 --> 15:18.436 align:middle
Just a follow-up.

15:18.966 --> 15:21.686 align:middle
The new leadership also
seem rather lukewarm

15:21.686 --> 15:24.026 align:middle
on press conferences
and on the dot plots.

15:24.026 --> 15:28.236 align:middle
What would your advice to him
be on these communication tools?

15:28.336 --> 15:28.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:28.876 --> 15:32.306 align:middle
So I'm not going to give him any
advice through you today here.

15:32.376 --> 15:36.216 align:middle
But I've-you know, I think
communications generally is-I

15:36.536 --> 15:39.756 align:middle
think every incoming Chair
takes a look at communications,

15:39.756 --> 15:40.846 align:middle
and it's a very healthy thing.

15:41.116 --> 15:45.056 align:middle
I mean, communications is-it's
very complex, and, you know,

15:45.056 --> 15:47.436 align:middle
you can always be
looking at new things.

15:47.436 --> 15:50.366 align:middle
And if that happens-it
feels like it's going

15:50.366 --> 15:52.126 align:middle
to happen-that's a
completely appropriate thing.

15:53.506 --> 15:53.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:53.976 --> 15:55.716 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

15:56.626 --> 15:57.786 align:middle
Hi, Chair, Chair Powell.

15:57.786 --> 15:58.816 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

15:58.816 --> 16:01.446 align:middle
Can you tell us if
you've been in touch

16:01.516 --> 16:02.946 align:middle
with incoming Chairman Warsh?

16:02.946 --> 16:07.416 align:middle
To what extent is this a
normal transition process

16:07.646 --> 16:10.546 align:middle
versus all the things swirling
around something unusual,

16:10.546 --> 16:12.756 align:middle
and what can we expect
when he takes,

16:13.006 --> 16:14.186 align:middle
takes that podium
in a few weeks?

16:14.186 --> 16:14.496 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:14.496 --> 16:18.246 align:middle
I haven't seen him since seeing
him at a dinner in January,

16:18.246 --> 16:20.786 align:middle
where I congratulated him
and had a nice chat with him.

16:21.256 --> 16:21.726 align:middle
Haven't seen him.

16:21.726 --> 16:23.096 align:middle
I don't know what a
normal process is.

16:23.096 --> 16:26.236 align:middle
You know, the last process
was with Janet Yellen,

16:26.236 --> 16:27.866 align:middle
with whom I had worked
for six years.

16:27.866 --> 16:30.426 align:middle
And so it was-you know, we
were sitting down the hall

16:30.426 --> 16:31.936 align:middle
from each other, so it was
a very different thing.

16:32.006 --> 16:35.836 align:middle
I think this is and will be
a very normal, standard kind

16:35.836 --> 16:37.416 align:middle
of a-kind of a transition
process.

16:37.416 --> 16:38.606 align:middle
So that's what I expect.

16:38.606 --> 16:39.796 align:middle
I have every reason
to think it will be.

16:39.796 --> 16:40.076 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

16:40.076 --> 16:40.486 align:middle
Quick follow-up.

16:40.486 --> 16:42.936 align:middle
Is the Supreme Court ruling
on Governor Cook a factor

16:42.936 --> 16:44.096 align:middle
in when you may leave
as a Governor?

16:44.356 --> 16:45.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:46.076 --> 16:47.446 align:middle
I wasn't thinking of it as such.

16:47.606 --> 16:48.786 align:middle
But, no, not really.

16:48.986 --> 16:51.646 align:middle
I mean, I'm thinking more of
the other things I mentioned.

16:52.386 --> 16:52.656 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:54.006 --> 16:56.566 align:middle
Chris. CHRIS RUGABER.

16:56.766 --> 16:58.506 align:middle
Hi. Thanks for taking
our question.

16:58.956 --> 17:00.156 align:middle
I wanted to ask a question

17:00.156 --> 17:04.196 align:middle
about your tenure-and Chris
Rugaber at Associated Press.

17:04.406 --> 17:06.296 align:middle
During your tenure as
Chair, you often spoke

17:06.296 --> 17:09.096 align:middle
about how disadvantaged
Americans benefited

17:09.096 --> 17:11.106 align:middle
from extended periods
of low unemployment.

17:11.596 --> 17:14.226 align:middle
And the new framework
the Fed adopted in 2020,

17:14.436 --> 17:17.286 align:middle
some economists say, elevated
the Fed's employment mandate.

17:17.886 --> 17:20.976 align:middle
Are you worried that the
pandemic inflation spike

17:21.046 --> 17:24.466 align:middle
that followed will make future
Fed Chairs more reluctant

17:24.496 --> 17:26.216 align:middle
to pursue a "hot" jobs market?

17:26.506 --> 17:27.346 align:middle
And should they be?

17:29.806 --> 17:30.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:30.416 --> 17:32.886 align:middle
I don't know the answer to that.

17:33.066 --> 17:36.176 align:middle
I mean, what we-so
what we experienced in,

17:37.876 --> 17:43.966 align:middle
in the teens-the mid-teens
was really low levels

17:43.966 --> 17:46.906 align:middle
of unemployment for
a long period of time

17:47.036 --> 17:48.646 align:middle
and no reaction from inflation.

17:48.996 --> 17:51.336 align:middle
Now, we all took-very
much took notice of that.

17:51.846 --> 17:56.486 align:middle
We also noticed that the biggest
wage gains were going to people

17:56.486 --> 17:58.366 align:middle
at the bottom end of
the income spectrum.

17:58.936 --> 18:01.956 align:middle
And we had many, many
reports of-I mean,

18:01.956 --> 18:03.966 align:middle
it felt like a fairly
stable equilibrium.

18:04.026 --> 18:05.976 align:middle
And a lot of benefits
were flowing to people

18:05.976 --> 18:08.376 align:middle
at the bottom end of
the income spectrum,

18:08.376 --> 18:11.786 align:middle
including companies were,
you know, setting up in, in,

18:11.786 --> 18:16.586 align:middle
you know-people who
were confined and, like,

18:16.586 --> 18:18.116 align:middle
training them before
they got out.

18:18.276 --> 18:19.846 align:middle
And it was a-it was
a very healthy sort

18:19.846 --> 18:21.346 align:middle
of set of societal dynamics.

18:21.816 --> 18:25.846 align:middle
So, of course, I think anybody
would love to get back to that.

18:25.996 --> 18:29.866 align:middle
I don't think that
anything that happened

18:29.866 --> 18:34.136 align:middle
to create the global pandemic
inflation was in any way related

18:34.136 --> 18:36.346 align:middle
to overweighting the
employment market.

18:36.346 --> 18:40.076 align:middle
I mean, it was a global shock
that happened essentially very,

18:40.076 --> 18:42.336 align:middle
very similarly all over the
world [and] that had to do

18:42.336 --> 18:45.486 align:middle
with closing and reopening,
stimulus, and all that.

18:45.486 --> 18:49.076 align:middle
And, I mean, you could look
at a graph of 10 big economies

18:49.076 --> 18:50.996 align:middle
on the page and not
know which was the U.S.

18:51.136 --> 18:53.126 align:middle
and which was Germany,
France, and things like that.

18:53.126 --> 18:56.836 align:middle
So I don't think
that that insight was

18:56.836 --> 18:59.796 align:middle
in any way responsible
for the high inflation

18:59.796 --> 19:00.586 align:middle
that we experienced.

19:01.146 --> 19:03.626 align:middle
So, I mean, I think it's
always been a balance.

19:03.626 --> 19:08.876 align:middle
You've got to-you've
got to be strong

19:08.876 --> 19:10.236 align:middle
on both of our dual mandates.

19:10.756 --> 19:13.726 align:middle
And we just-for example,
now we don't feel

19:13.786 --> 19:16.626 align:middle
that the labor market is at
all a source of inflation,

19:16.626 --> 19:17.856 align:middle
so we don't need to be
worrying about that.

19:17.856 --> 19:19.796 align:middle
It's been a long time since we
have had to worry about that.

19:20.306 --> 19:22.246 align:middle
Well, actually, during
the pandemic recovery,

19:22.246 --> 19:25.686 align:middle
the labor market was super
overheated and tight,

19:25.686 --> 19:26.946 align:middle
and that's when we
had to worry about it.

19:26.996 --> 19:27.866 align:middle
But not now.

19:28.446 --> 19:28.866 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

19:28.866 --> 19:30.156 align:middle
Just on the other issue,

19:30.156 --> 19:33.586 align:middle
are you-would you need-do
you need more assurance

19:33.586 --> 19:35.796 align:middle
from the Justice Department
before stepping down?

19:35.796 --> 19:38.656 align:middle
Is that what you're waiting
for, or what else is going

19:38.656 --> 19:39.296 align:middle
to affect- CHAIR POWELL.

19:39.396 --> 19:41.726 align:middle
I'm waiting, you know, for
the investigation to be well

19:41.726 --> 19:44.436 align:middle
and truly over with
finality and transparency.

19:45.346 --> 19:46.506 align:middle
I'm waiting for that.

19:46.506 --> 19:50.146 align:middle
And I will leave when I think
it's appropriate to do so.

19:51.746 --> 19:52.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:52.326 --> 19:52.836 align:middle
Michael McKee.

19:55.216 --> 19:56.276 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

19:56.626 --> 19:58.426 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

19:58.466 --> 20:01.076 align:middle
I'd like to ask you, if you
could, explain a little more

20:01.076 --> 20:03.026 align:middle
or characterize a little
more the discussion

20:03.026 --> 20:06.586 align:middle
about the two-sided
view and interest rates,

20:06.586 --> 20:08.186 align:middle
because there were some members

20:08.186 --> 20:10.156 align:middle
of the Open Market Committee
who've been suggesting

20:10.156 --> 20:13.916 align:middle
that we may need to raise
interest rates even absent the

20:13.916 --> 20:18.046 align:middle
war because inflation was
not coming down fast enough.

20:18.286 --> 20:22.356 align:middle
Is there any sense that interest
rates might have to go up,

20:22.356 --> 20:26.996 align:middle
or was this just a set-up
to sort of warn people

20:27.126 --> 20:29.626 align:middle
that you're worried
about the war impacts?

20:30.266 --> 20:31.176 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:31.366 --> 20:33.636 align:middle
So nobody-the three
dissenters and others

20:33.636 --> 20:36.796 align:middle
who could have supported that
and others who were, you know,

20:36.796 --> 20:38.506 align:middle
who were voters and
prefer-nonvoters

20:38.506 --> 20:41.226 align:middle
who preferred it, they all
supported the rate decision,

20:41.446 --> 20:41.646 align:middle
right?

20:41.646 --> 20:44.046 align:middle
So people are not saying
we need to hike now.

20:44.156 --> 20:47.906 align:middle
It's more a question of, you
know, don't we kind of feel

20:48.176 --> 20:49.486 align:middle
that we should be neutral?

20:49.556 --> 20:51.886 align:middle
And markets are-markets-what
are markets doing?

20:51.996 --> 20:53.996 align:middle
People argue that
this is consistent

20:53.996 --> 20:54.816 align:middle
with markets are doing.

20:55.566 --> 20:57.986 align:middle
And, again, it's a
very fair question.

20:57.986 --> 21:00.616 align:middle
But, you know, these
changes, there is-you know,

21:00.616 --> 21:02.166 align:middle
it's a form of forward guidance.

21:02.736 --> 21:05.876 align:middle
And you want to make-you don't
want to make them-you want

21:05.876 --> 21:08.426 align:middle
to make them in a way that
will be sustained and continue

21:08.426 --> 21:11.346 align:middle
to make sense and not
something you need to take back,

21:11.346 --> 21:12.546 align:middle
you know, fairly quickly.

21:12.546 --> 21:16.746 align:middle
So I think we just-a
group of us, including me,

21:16.746 --> 21:18.626 align:middle
didn't feel like we needed
to be in a hurry on that,

21:18.986 --> 21:21.886 align:middle
that markets are not confused
about our reaction function.

21:21.886 --> 21:23.386 align:middle
We don't have a problem
to solve on that.

21:23.746 --> 21:24.366 align:middle
But the other side

21:24.366 --> 21:26.616 align:middle
of the argument is a good
argument, too, as I mentioned.

21:26.616 --> 21:29.496 align:middle
It's a perfectly good
argument to be having,

21:29.596 --> 21:30.966 align:middle
good discussion to
be having, so.

21:30.966 --> 21:32.456 align:middle
And it came out the
way it came out.

21:32.896 --> 21:33.336 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

21:33.336 --> 21:39.036 align:middle
Well, you've got three dissents
in favor of a two-sided warning.

21:39.036 --> 21:41.476 align:middle
You've got yourself
staying on the Board.

21:42.016 --> 21:44.186 align:middle
You've got the criticism
that does come

21:44.186 --> 21:45.336 align:middle
from elected officials.

21:45.786 --> 21:49.736 align:middle
And you've got a lot of
critics who have faulted the Fed

21:50.006 --> 21:53.456 align:middle
for being too slow-2021-with
inflation.

21:53.866 --> 21:57.476 align:middle
Are you worried about Fed
credibility under all of this?

21:57.476 --> 21:59.416 align:middle
Is that one reason that
you want to stay on?

22:01.336 --> 22:01.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:01.716 --> 22:03.456 align:middle
Not driving my thinking now.

22:03.456 --> 22:05.356 align:middle
I mean, monetary policy is going

22:05.356 --> 22:08.556 align:middle
to get made by, you
know, 19 people.

22:09.616 --> 22:10.916 align:middle
There's a lot of
stability there.

22:11.196 --> 22:13.386 align:middle
I mean, if you think about it,

22:13.386 --> 22:17.796 align:middle
any-every new Fed Chair
has the same situation,

22:17.796 --> 22:20.796 align:middle
which is you've got 18
colleagues on the FOMC,

22:21.146 --> 22:23.286 align:middle
11 of them vote during any year,

22:23.326 --> 22:27.166 align:middle
and you have to-your job
is to create consensus.

22:27.676 --> 22:30.106 align:middle
It's to talk to them,
understand them, you know,

22:30.166 --> 22:33.366 align:middle
be inside their thinking, and
be able to pull them together

22:33.366 --> 22:35.306 align:middle
and get consensus and move.

22:35.466 --> 22:38.566 align:middle
And that's what every
Fed Chair has to do.

22:38.566 --> 22:42.306 align:middle
And I think Kevin Warsh is
actually quite well-he has the

22:42.306 --> 22:45.866 align:middle
capabilities, skills to be very
good at that, I would think.

22:46.326 --> 22:49.456 align:middle
So I think-I'm not so
worried about that process.

22:49.596 --> 22:52.126 align:middle
You know, I think
that'll work itself out.

22:53.046 --> 22:53.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:54.426 --> 22:56.796 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

22:56.796 --> 22:57.756 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

22:57.826 --> 22:58.976 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

22:59.336 --> 23:02.056 align:middle
You mentioned that,
staying on as a Governor,

23:02.056 --> 23:03.656 align:middle
you intend to keep
a low profile.

23:03.656 --> 23:05.556 align:middle
I'm just wondering if you could
give us a little more detail

23:05.556 --> 23:09.666 align:middle
on what that looks like and how
you can- [Laughter] Touché.

23:09.816 --> 23:16.096 align:middle
What that-yeah, walk down the
steps-what that looks like.

23:16.096 --> 23:17.976 align:middle
And, particularly around
the policy discussion,

23:18.056 --> 23:22.996 align:middle
how you're able to have your
intervention and not be a

23:22.996 --> 23:24.576 align:middle
"shadow Chair" and not have kind

23:24.576 --> 23:26.216 align:middle
of an outsized influence
over the process.

23:26.216 --> 23:26.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:26.556 --> 23:28.846 align:middle
Yeah. You know, that's just
something I would never do-you

23:28.846 --> 23:30.056 align:middle
know, the "shadow Chair" thing.

23:30.056 --> 23:33.276 align:middle
No. You know, I-it's-I don't
know what the exact specifics

23:33.276 --> 23:36.436 align:middle
of it will be, but I'm going
back to being a Governor.

23:36.656 --> 23:38.036 align:middle
I respect the role of Chair.

23:38.276 --> 23:41.506 align:middle
You know, I was a Chair-I
was a Governor for six years,

23:41.506 --> 23:42.506 align:middle
and I know what that's like.

23:42.506 --> 23:46.256 align:middle
I know what-and I had a, you
know, pretty front row seat,

23:46.256 --> 23:49.226 align:middle
particularly with Chair
Yellen, to whom I was close.

23:49.226 --> 23:53.086 align:middle
I, I worked with Chairman
Bernanke for two years, but,

23:53.596 --> 23:55.576 align:middle
you know, I was brand
new at that time.

23:55.576 --> 23:56.876 align:middle
So I got a sense of what it was.

23:56.876 --> 24:00.306 align:middle
And I had real sympathy
for how hard it is to get

24:00.306 --> 24:01.646 align:middle
that group to consensus.

24:01.736 --> 24:03.906 align:middle
And I always felt like I-you
know, I don't want to add

24:03.906 --> 24:04.976 align:middle
to that unnecessarily.

24:04.976 --> 24:07.106 align:middle
And that means try
to support the Chairs

24:07.656 --> 24:09.286 align:middle
where the Chair-the
direction the Chair wants

24:09.286 --> 24:10.126 align:middle
to go, if you can.

24:10.256 --> 24:11.146 align:middle
If you can't, you can't.

24:11.566 --> 24:13.696 align:middle
But-and I think that's the
way it's always worked there

24:13.696 --> 24:16.486 align:middle
because the Chair only has
one vote plus the ability

24:16.486 --> 24:17.856 align:middle
to develop consensus.

24:17.906 --> 24:20.286 align:middle
And if people won't
be, you know,

24:20.286 --> 24:22.316 align:middle
they won't-if they're
not flexible at all,

24:22.316 --> 24:23.386 align:middle
then how do you ever do that?

24:23.386 --> 24:26.076 align:middle
And so that's why the Chair has
the authority, the Chair has,

24:26.176 --> 24:29.816 align:middle
really, is to develop
relationships with people

24:29.816 --> 24:32.276 align:middle
and work with them, and then-and
then put something forward

24:32.276 --> 24:33.306 align:middle
that has consensus.

24:33.666 --> 24:37.646 align:middle
And I, you know, I propose to be
a very constructive participant

24:37.646 --> 24:40.616 align:middle
in that process,
really out of respect

24:40.616 --> 24:41.836 align:middle
for the office of the Chair.

24:42.606 --> 24:43.276 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

24:43.486 --> 24:46.966 align:middle
And in your view as a
soon-to-be Governor,

24:47.456 --> 24:50.216 align:middle
how do you see the risks
of oil prices bleeding

24:50.216 --> 24:52.466 align:middle
into core inflation
in coming weeks?

24:52.466 --> 24:55.856 align:middle
Because that was-it seems like
the commentary that was coming

24:55.856 --> 24:57.726 align:middle
from particularly some of
the Reserve Bank presidents,

24:57.756 --> 25:00.986 align:middle
there were elevated concerns
about the bleed into core, and,

25:01.056 --> 25:02.796 align:middle
you know, here we are
with three dissents now.

25:02.796 --> 25:05.276 align:middle
What do you see as the prospect
of a core inflation problem?

25:05.276 --> 25:05.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:05.606 --> 25:07.566 align:middle
You know, those prospects
are real.

25:07.696 --> 25:10.566 align:middle
Remember, though,
our-they're real.

25:10.566 --> 25:12.576 align:middle
And the real thing is we're
going to have to wait and see.

25:13.016 --> 25:13.906 align:middle
We're going to need to see.

25:13.906 --> 25:16.966 align:middle
And the good news is we think
our policy stance is just-is

25:16.966 --> 25:19.166 align:middle
in a very good place
for us to wait and see.

25:19.566 --> 25:22.896 align:middle
We, you know, we're right kind
of at the high end of neutral

25:22.986 --> 25:24.766 align:middle
or perhaps mildly restrictive.

25:25.386 --> 25:29.306 align:middle
The labor market shows more
and more signs of stability,

25:29.756 --> 25:31.636 align:middle
whereas inflation is
kind of misbehaving.

25:31.636 --> 25:35.226 align:middle
And so maybe a little bit of
restriction or the high end

25:35.226 --> 25:36.926 align:middle
of neutral is just
the right place to be.

25:36.926 --> 25:40.836 align:middle
So we can wait here and
see-and see how things work

25:40.836 --> 25:41.756 align:middle
out before we act.

25:42.446 --> 25:44.336 align:middle
And we'll see how
much that-you know,

25:44.336 --> 25:45.926 align:middle
how much does come
through into core.

25:45.926 --> 25:47.946 align:middle
You see it already in
airfares, of course.

25:47.996 --> 25:50.196 align:middle
But you may see it
in many other places.

25:51.286 --> 25:53.246 align:middle
You know, we just
don't know yet.

25:53.246 --> 25:56.176 align:middle
And it's so unknowable,
because how long will the Strait

25:56.216 --> 25:56.696 align:middle
be closed?

25:56.696 --> 25:59.956 align:middle
You can develop any number
of scenarios that you want,

26:00.376 --> 26:01.916 align:middle
but we really won't
know till we know.

26:01.916 --> 26:04.456 align:middle
So, fortunately, we're
in a good place to wait

26:04.456 --> 26:05.586 align:middle
and let things develop.

26:05.586 --> 26:06.026 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

26:07.536 --> 26:07.996 align:middle
Thank you.

26:07.996 --> 26:08.556 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:09.436 --> 26:11.556 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

26:11.556 --> 26:12.936 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

26:12.936 --> 26:15.786 align:middle
You started holding
postmeeting press conferences

26:15.786 --> 26:18.516 align:middle
for every meeting as opposed to
the ones with just-with SEPs.

26:18.516 --> 26:21.356 align:middle
Can you talk about why you
see that as a net positive?

26:21.916 --> 26:22.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:22.466 --> 26:26.586 align:middle
So we, we always said-when
we were doing quarterly press

26:26.586 --> 26:28.086 align:middle
conferences, we always
said "we can move

26:28.086 --> 26:29.486 align:middle
at any meeting"-but
we only ever moved

26:29.486 --> 26:31.056 align:middle
at the quarterly SEP meetings

26:31.056 --> 26:32.066 align:middle
where we had the
press conference.

26:32.546 --> 26:36.176 align:middle
So if you think about it, you
know, we-during the pandemic,

26:36.176 --> 26:38.426 align:middle
we were moving, like,
a lot at every meeting

26:38.426 --> 26:39.926 align:middle
and sometimes between meetings.

26:40.016 --> 26:41.776 align:middle
And doing that without a
press conference, I think,

26:41.776 --> 26:42.726 align:middle
would have been quite
challenging.

26:43.086 --> 26:44.616 align:middle
It's become the industry norm.

26:45.246 --> 26:46.076 align:middle
It's the standard.

26:46.586 --> 26:48.676 align:middle
I don't know whether that
has to remain that way.

26:48.756 --> 26:49.146 align:middle
I don't know.

26:49.146 --> 26:51.716 align:middle
I mean, I-it's just something
people have become used to.

26:51.716 --> 26:55.706 align:middle
And I do think it's quite
helpful to, you know, to-I mean,

26:55.866 --> 26:59.736 align:middle
I try to deliver a message on
behalf of the Committee rather

26:59.736 --> 27:01.976 align:middle
than 18 people-18
other people going out

27:01.976 --> 27:03.116 align:middle
and delivering their message.

27:03.116 --> 27:04.766 align:middle
And it's, you know, it's
going to be all over the place

27:04.766 --> 27:08.146 align:middle
because we do, thankfully,
have widely disparate views.

27:09.226 --> 27:09.846 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

27:09.926 --> 27:10.526 align:middle
Okay, thanks.

27:10.526 --> 27:11.836 align:middle
The other thing I wanted to ask

27:11.836 --> 27:14.146 align:middle
about was the communications
review from last year.

27:15.366 --> 27:18.046 align:middle
Could you describe
the debate last year,

27:18.046 --> 27:20.176 align:middle
what changes were
considered, what you wanted,

27:20.506 --> 27:25.636 align:middle
and what prevented action-any
action on the-on those changes?

27:25.636 --> 27:25.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:25.846 --> 27:29.586 align:middle
So I'm not going to go into
the real small specifics.

27:29.636 --> 27:36.306 align:middle
But what we found very
quickly was that making,

27:36.566 --> 27:40.136 align:middle
making changes-making really
large changes, for example,

27:40.136 --> 27:45.706 align:middle
to the dot plot or the SEP, it
didn't have-we couldn't come

27:45.706 --> 27:48.366 align:middle
up with anything that had really
broad support on the Committee.

27:49.196 --> 27:50.736 align:middle
And so we just moved on.

27:50.736 --> 27:54.866 align:middle
We didn't-we didn't really do as
much on that as we might have.

27:55.596 --> 27:57.646 align:middle
And, you know, I was never
the world's biggest fan

27:57.646 --> 28:00.276 align:middle
of the dot plot, but you
can't-you can't beat something

28:00.276 --> 28:00.816 align:middle
with nothing.

28:00.816 --> 28:03.476 align:middle
And, you know, there's a-that
we've looked at a bunch

28:03.476 --> 28:07.476 align:middle
of things and, you know,
it's something, like I said,

28:07.476 --> 28:10.366 align:middle
I think every new Chair is
going to look at our suite

28:10.366 --> 28:15.216 align:middle
of communications and think
about what would be changes.

28:15.216 --> 28:17.636 align:middle
We are the only major
central bank

28:17.636 --> 28:19.206 align:middle
that doesn't publish a forecast.

28:19.936 --> 28:22.466 align:middle
And that's because we have
a 19-person Committee, and,

28:22.466 --> 28:25.296 align:middle
you know, you try to do it-you
try to do that at the Board,

28:25.296 --> 28:27.146 align:middle
that's hard-at the
Committee, that's hard.

28:27.146 --> 28:29.336 align:middle
It's hard if you
do it at the staff.

28:29.336 --> 28:31.666 align:middle
So it's, you know,
it's been-it works.

28:31.666 --> 28:34.976 align:middle
I think our communications are
fine, but looking at doing it

28:34.976 --> 28:37.166 align:middle
in a different and better
way is the most natural thing

28:37.166 --> 28:37.556 align:middle
in the world.

28:39.736 --> 28:40.036 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:40.066 --> 28:41.916 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

28:44.036 --> 28:44.576 align:middle
Thank you.

28:44.866 --> 28:46.236 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

28:46.236 --> 28:48.616 align:middle
If I could follow up on
Mike's question about hikes,

28:48.996 --> 28:51.326 align:middle
are we right to assume
that the hawkish outcome

28:51.326 --> 28:52.836 align:middle
for the Fed is still one

28:52.836 --> 28:56.416 align:middle
in which the Committee just
extends the pause in rate cuts?

28:56.536 --> 28:58.756 align:middle
And to what extent is
there a growing sense

28:58.756 --> 28:59.536 align:middle
within the Committee

28:59.536 --> 29:02.766 align:middle
that monetary policy really
isn't just restrictive

29:02.766 --> 29:04.146 align:middle
at all right now?

29:04.146 --> 29:05.756 align:middle
The economy is holding

29:05.756 --> 29:08.576 align:middle
up relatively well despite
this major energy shock,

29:08.756 --> 29:10.556 align:middle
the unemployment rate
has ticked lower,

29:10.736 --> 29:13.616 align:middle
inflation was moving
sideways even before the war

29:13.616 --> 29:14.776 align:middle
and is now moving higher.

29:15.366 --> 29:17.326 align:middle
So where is the Committee
at on that debate?

29:17.396 --> 29:17.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:17.736 --> 29:20.326 align:middle
You know, where we're at
is we think our-really,

29:20.326 --> 29:23.236 align:middle
we think our policy
rate is in a good place.

29:23.386 --> 29:25.836 align:middle
If we need to hike, we
will certainly signal that,

29:25.836 --> 29:27.386 align:middle
and we will-and we
will certainly do it.

29:27.926 --> 29:30.926 align:middle
And if we need to cut, then-or
if it's appropriate to cut,

29:30.926 --> 29:32.256 align:middle
then we'll signal the opposite.

29:32.756 --> 29:36.866 align:middle
I think we-because we feel like
we're in a good place to move

29:37.436 --> 29:42.076 align:middle
in either direction, nobody's
calling for a hike right now.

29:42.706 --> 29:47.026 align:middle
So it really is going to depend
on how things-how things evolve.

29:47.696 --> 29:49.336 align:middle
And, you know, that's
really where it is.

29:49.436 --> 29:54.336 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, you know,
much closer question this cycle

29:54.336 --> 29:57.066 align:middle
on changing the guidance,
but, ultimately, we didn't.

29:57.066 --> 29:57.926 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

29:57.926 --> 29:59.466 align:middle
And, as it relates to the war,

29:59.466 --> 30:02.956 align:middle
at what point do you think the
risk to growth will be larger

30:02.956 --> 30:05.876 align:middle
than the risk to inflation
as this conflict drags on?

30:06.416 --> 30:07.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:07.676 --> 30:10.516 align:middle
You know, you just have to
find that out empirically.

30:11.216 --> 30:15.086 align:middle
You know, with-given our-the
fact that we're, you know,

30:15.086 --> 30:16.536 align:middle
a big exporter of energy

30:16.536 --> 30:20.816 align:middle
and that our economy is far less
energy intensive, oil intensive,

30:20.816 --> 30:23.316 align:middle
than it was during
the '70s, you know,

30:23.546 --> 30:27.436 align:middle
the effects on the United States
are really substantially less

30:27.676 --> 30:30.936 align:middle
than those of Western
Europe or Asia.

30:31.236 --> 30:32.806 align:middle
We're feeling much
greater effects

30:32.946 --> 30:36.556 align:middle
from these things-the effects
we're feeling in, you know,

30:36.556 --> 30:40.296 align:middle
in the current situation
currently and in sort

30:40.296 --> 30:42.526 align:middle
of what's priced in,
which is, you know,

30:42.666 --> 30:44.346 align:middle
a relatively quick outcome.

30:45.066 --> 30:48.056 align:middle
If this goes on for much longer
and prices go much higher,

30:48.056 --> 30:49.326 align:middle
then we'll feel that much more.

30:49.756 --> 30:51.846 align:middle
And, of course, I'm talking

30:51.846 --> 30:53.126 align:middle
about aggregate inflation
numbers.

30:53.126 --> 30:54.876 align:middle
We know-we're very well aware

30:55.066 --> 30:58.246 align:middle
that people are experiencing
higher gas prices all

30:58.246 --> 30:59.126 align:middle
over the country now.

30:59.576 --> 31:00.636 align:middle
And that hurts.

31:00.686 --> 31:03.336 align:middle
That's-and these are-those
hikes may continue to happen.

31:03.966 --> 31:05.746 align:middle
And other things
are going to start

31:05.746 --> 31:09.576 align:middle
to reflect-airline fares I've
mentioned and other products

31:09.576 --> 31:12.496 align:middle
and services that are
dependent upon petroleum

31:12.496 --> 31:13.596 align:middle
and derivatives of petroleum.

31:13.596 --> 31:15.116 align:middle
People are going to
start to feel that.

31:15.116 --> 31:17.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:17.386 --> 31:19.916 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

31:19.966 --> 31:20.376 align:middle
Thank you.

31:20.376 --> 31:21.046 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

31:21.046 --> 31:22.526 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

31:22.976 --> 31:24.956 align:middle
So I guess I'll just ask
you directly on this.

31:24.956 --> 31:27.116 align:middle
The markets don't see a rate cut

31:27.566 --> 31:29.316 align:middle
at all this year-is
what they're predicting.

31:29.656 --> 31:31.586 align:middle
Do you think that we
are at the neutral rate?

31:31.626 --> 31:32.506 align:middle
Why or why not?

31:33.826 --> 31:34.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:34.306 --> 31:37.366 align:middle
You know, the neutral rate
is a-we cannot know it with,

31:37.646 --> 31:38.516 align:middle
with certainty.

31:38.516 --> 31:41.026 align:middle
I think pretty close to
the neutral rate, yeah.

31:41.026 --> 31:43.906 align:middle
I always had it, you know,
between 3 and 4 percent.

31:43.906 --> 31:46.636 align:middle
We're a little north of 3½
[percent], so that's well

31:46.636 --> 31:48.906 align:middle
in the range of what I
would consider reasonable,

31:48.906 --> 31:50.246 align:middle
but at the higher
end of the range

31:50.246 --> 31:52.456 align:middle
of what I would consider
reasonable neutral rate.

31:52.986 --> 31:57.326 align:middle
You know, I think you're-the
labor market is still probably

31:57.356 --> 31:58.816 align:middle
cooling off just a little bit.

31:58.816 --> 32:05.256 align:middle
And I don't think there's
much of a case for-any case,

32:05.256 --> 32:08.756 align:middle
really-for, for policy
looking, you know,

32:08.906 --> 32:10.076 align:middle
meaningfully restrictive.

32:10.126 --> 32:12.536 align:middle
Maybe mildly restrictive
or neutral, I would say.

32:12.536 --> 32:13.636 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

32:13.636 --> 32:15.486 align:middle
And I want to follow up on
some of the other questions

32:15.486 --> 32:17.116 align:middle
about your future a little bit.

32:17.436 --> 32:19.916 align:middle
The first time we've
seen four dissents now

32:20.136 --> 32:21.926 align:middle
since October of 1992.

32:22.376 --> 32:24.146 align:middle
Are you handing off
a divided Fed?

32:24.146 --> 32:25.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:25.416 --> 32:30.846 align:middle
You know, the thing to remember
is we have always had vigorous

32:30.846 --> 32:33.856 align:middle
debates, and they're
excellent debates.

32:33.856 --> 32:35.636 align:middle
I have to say, they're-they've
been really good.

32:36.316 --> 32:40.096 align:middle
And we're in an unusually
difficult situation.

32:40.546 --> 32:43.606 align:middle
So we've really had
four supply shocks.

32:43.606 --> 32:45.686 align:middle
You can-actually, you can
say more than four, but,

32:45.686 --> 32:49.936 align:middle
at a minimum, we've-we had the
pandemic, we had the invasion

32:50.076 --> 32:54.816 align:middle
of Ukraine, we had tariffs, and
now we have Iran and the oil,

32:55.256 --> 32:57.156 align:middle
you know, the oil spike.

32:57.696 --> 33:02.196 align:middle
So those-every, every supply
shock has the capability of,

33:02.196 --> 33:04.646 align:middle
right, driving inflation
up and unemployment up.

33:04.646 --> 33:06.056 align:middle
And what do you do?

33:06.056 --> 33:08.766 align:middle
You know, you're-it's-the
central bank has a really hard

33:08.766 --> 33:09.626 align:middle
time knowing what to do.

33:09.626 --> 33:12.956 align:middle
So the right thing to do is to
try to balance the achievement

33:12.956 --> 33:14.956 align:middle
of those two goals, and that's
what our framework calls

33:14.956 --> 33:15.516 align:middle
for us to do.

33:16.146 --> 33:19.096 align:middle
But these are really
tough, difficult judgments.

33:19.096 --> 33:21.006 align:middle
You've got to have a
forecast for each variable.

33:21.006 --> 33:22.936 align:middle
You've got to think how
long it's going to take

33:22.936 --> 33:24.086 align:middle
to get back to target.

33:24.606 --> 33:27.406 align:middle
You've got to think how
restrictive or not is policy.

33:27.406 --> 33:29.936 align:middle
So it's only natural
that you have a range

33:29.936 --> 33:30.796 align:middle
of views on the Committee.

33:31.186 --> 33:32.596 align:middle
You know, people are going
to see it different ways,

33:32.596 --> 33:34.076 align:middle
they're going to have
different risk tolerances

33:34.076 --> 33:34.956 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

33:35.246 --> 33:36.426 align:middle
I mean, if everybody agreed,

33:36.426 --> 33:38.836 align:middle
that would be-that
would be surprising.

33:39.126 --> 33:42.566 align:middle
And I think it's
only-it's partly a function

33:42.566 --> 33:46.836 align:middle
of the extraordinarily
challenging set of supply shocks

33:46.836 --> 33:49.086 align:middle
that we've been dealing with
now for five, six years.

33:49.666 --> 33:51.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:51.346 --> 33:54.386 align:middle
Selina. SELINA WANG.

33:54.866 --> 33:55.996 align:middle
Thank you so much, Chair Powell.

33:55.996 --> 33:57.466 align:middle
Selina Wang with ABC News.

33:57.606 --> 33:59.916 align:middle
Are you confident that
Kevin Warsh will stand

33:59.916 --> 34:01.906 align:middle
up to political pressure
from President Trump?

34:01.906 --> 34:02.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:02.716 --> 34:06.176 align:middle
So he testified very strongly
to that effect in his hearing,

34:06.176 --> 34:07.636 align:middle
and I'll take him at his word.

34:07.636 --> 34:08.286 align:middle
SELINA WANG.

34:08.556 --> 34:11.296 align:middle
And when it comes to gas, right
now it's over $4 a gallon.

34:11.546 --> 34:13.976 align:middle
Inflation just hit
a two-year high.

34:14.236 --> 34:17.456 align:middle
Should Americans expect to
be paying higher gas prices

34:17.456 --> 34:18.636 align:middle
for the rest of this year?

34:18.636 --> 34:19.776 align:middle
And, in your view, does

34:19.806 --> 34:22.376 align:middle
that take a rate cut
off of the table?

34:22.626 --> 34:26.026 align:middle
And, secondly, by staying
on as Fed Governor,

34:26.026 --> 34:28.176 align:middle
what message do you think
you're sending to the President?

34:28.296 --> 34:28.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:28.706 --> 34:31.196 align:middle
I don't know what gas
prices are going to do

34:31.196 --> 34:31.886 align:middle
for the rest of the year.

34:32.536 --> 34:36.096 align:middle
And it will depend on how
long the Strait remains closed

34:36.096 --> 34:38.116 align:middle
and how quickly it can be
reopened and that kind of thing.

34:38.366 --> 34:41.746 align:middle
But, remember, when
gas prices go up,

34:42.066 --> 34:44.756 align:middle
that's disposable income
coming out of people's pockets,

34:44.756 --> 34:46.366 align:middle
so they're going to spend
less on other things.

34:46.366 --> 34:48.236 align:middle
So there will be a hit to GDP.

34:48.616 --> 34:52.896 align:middle
So it's a, you know, it's a
question whether spending,

34:52.896 --> 34:57.016 align:middle
you know, goes down to offset
the inflationary effects.

34:57.246 --> 35:02.436 align:middle
So it's not-the answer isn't
obvious ex ante whether

35:02.436 --> 35:05.746 align:middle
you-whether you should move
your rate over because of that.

35:06.656 --> 35:07.806 align:middle
We'll have to see
how it evolves.

35:08.226 --> 35:08.506 align:middle
SELINA WANG.

35:08.506 --> 35:10.716 align:middle
And the message it sends to
President Trump by staying on?

35:10.716 --> 35:10.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:10.946 --> 35:12.286 align:middle
I'll stand on what
I said earlier.

35:13.016 --> 35:13.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:15.246 --> 35:18.616 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

35:18.796 --> 35:20.326 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

35:20.836 --> 35:23.596 align:middle
During your tenure, Fed
independence has come

35:23.596 --> 35:25.636 align:middle
under pressure in a
lot of different ways.

35:25.996 --> 35:28.496 align:middle
And I was just wondering,
practically speaking,

35:28.706 --> 35:31.536 align:middle
where do you see Fed
independence as coming from?

35:31.536 --> 35:32.636 align:middle
Is it the law?

35:32.806 --> 35:35.076 align:middle
Is it political support
from Congress?

35:35.076 --> 35:36.566 align:middle
Is it the actions of the Fed?

35:37.006 --> 35:39.496 align:middle
What sustains Fed independence?

35:39.496 --> 35:40.026 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:40.026 --> 35:42.616 align:middle
Well, it's, you know,
it's-to a significant extent,

35:42.616 --> 35:43.326 align:middle
it is the law.

35:43.806 --> 35:45.286 align:middle
And, you know, we've had to go

35:45.286 --> 35:49.326 align:middle
to court to-successfully,
so far-to defend it.

35:49.376 --> 35:53.826 align:middle
But, you know, the law
does create a setting

35:53.826 --> 35:59.696 align:middle
in which the Fed can and is
directed to make monetary policy

35:59.696 --> 36:01.806 align:middle
without consideration
of political factors.

36:02.306 --> 36:05.186 align:middle
And so part of it is the law.

36:05.266 --> 36:06.916 align:middle
But it goes beyond that, though.

36:06.916 --> 36:08.016 align:middle
There's a set of customs.

36:08.016 --> 36:12.146 align:middle
There's a boundary line between
the Fed and the Administration,

36:12.146 --> 36:13.806 align:middle
between the Fed and the
Treasury Department.

36:14.226 --> 36:15.936 align:middle
And we need to respect
those-continue

36:15.936 --> 36:17.336 align:middle
to respect those boundaries

36:17.936 --> 36:19.716 align:middle
about what the Fed
is responsible for

36:19.716 --> 36:21.336 align:middle
and what the Treasury
is responsible for

36:21.836 --> 36:24.436 align:middle
and what the rest of the
Administration's responsible.

36:24.436 --> 36:25.786 align:middle
So some of it is legal.

36:26.316 --> 36:28.376 align:middle
In fact, it's all legal
at the end of the day.

36:28.376 --> 36:30.576 align:middle
But there-it's more than
just monetary policy.

36:30.576 --> 36:32.856 align:middle
We, you know, we
don't-we don't want

36:33.016 --> 36:36.536 align:middle
to use our tools to-we
haven't wanted to use our tools

36:36.536 --> 36:40.166 align:middle
to achieve goals that are really
clearly outside our mandate.

36:40.356 --> 36:42.416 align:middle
Every Administration looks
at our tools and thinks

36:42.906 --> 36:45.906 align:middle
that would be good to repurpose
those to serve other purposes,

36:45.906 --> 36:49.256 align:middle
but that gets dragged-that's
dragging us into politics

36:49.256 --> 36:50.456 align:middle
and into fiscal policy.

36:50.456 --> 36:51.866 align:middle
So we've resisted that.

36:52.856 --> 36:53.246 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

36:53.246 --> 36:55.946 align:middle
Well, and maybe another way of
asking it, too, is, do you think

36:55.946 --> 36:57.786 align:middle
that Fed independence
is as strong now

36:57.936 --> 36:59.356 align:middle
as when you became Chair?

36:59.356 --> 37:01.416 align:middle
And, if so, why?

37:02.226 --> 37:02.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:02.766 --> 37:03.866 align:middle
Look, I think it's at risk.

37:04.016 --> 37:08.476 align:middle
I mean, I think these, these
you know, these legal assaults,

37:08.476 --> 37:10.626 align:middle
if you will-as I
mentioned, you know,

37:10.776 --> 37:13.536 align:middle
the institution is being
battered over these things.

37:13.536 --> 37:16.986 align:middle
We're having to resort to,
you know, the courts to,

37:17.096 --> 37:21.776 align:middle
to enforce our legal, you know,
it's not so much independence,

37:22.026 --> 37:26.426 align:middle
it's really the ability to do
monetary-to make monetary policy

37:26.426 --> 37:27.916 align:middle
without political
considerations.

37:28.526 --> 37:29.876 align:middle
That's what we're talking about.

37:30.066 --> 37:33.396 align:middle
And we've had to do that,
and we've been successful

37:33.396 --> 37:35.056 align:middle
so far, but that's not over.

37:35.056 --> 37:36.606 align:middle
None of that is concluded yet.

37:36.606 --> 37:37.986 align:middle
And it's, you know,
it's really important.

37:37.986 --> 37:40.956 align:middle
It's not about-it's not about
people who work at the Fed

37:40.956 --> 37:44.186 align:middle
or the institution-it's about
the benefits of a central bank

37:44.726 --> 37:48.176 align:middle
that makes decisions
based on analysis

37:48.396 --> 37:52.346 align:middle
and our best thinking
rather than, you know,

37:52.346 --> 37:53.976 align:middle
trying to help or
hurt politicians.

37:53.976 --> 37:58.106 align:middle
It's, you know-there's a bright
line between central banks

37:58.106 --> 37:59.366 align:middle
who do one and do the other.

37:59.416 --> 38:03.516 align:middle
And successful countries
have uniformly-successful,

38:03.516 --> 38:06.606 align:middle
advanced-economy countries
have a really strong set

38:06.606 --> 38:08.386 align:middle
of protections around
their central bank just

38:08.386 --> 38:09.006 align:middle
for that reason.

38:09.606 --> 38:11.036 align:middle
So that's what it's all about.

38:11.126 --> 38:13.656 align:middle
You know, I think-I
am confident,

38:13.656 --> 38:15.746 align:middle
as I said in my remarks,
that the Fed will continue

38:15.856 --> 38:17.926 align:middle
to make its decision based

38:17.926 --> 38:20.206 align:middle
on analysis-rigorous
analysis-and not

38:20.206 --> 38:22.926 align:middle
on political considerations,
but we've had to fight for it.

38:23.446 --> 38:25.876 align:middle
And I, you know, I'd like
to think that-you know,

38:25.876 --> 38:28.896 align:middle
I'd like to think we can get
out of that era and go back

38:28.896 --> 38:31.666 align:middle
to respecting, you
know, what the law says

38:31.666 --> 38:34.146 align:middle
and what custom has been,
which is to, you know,

38:34.146 --> 38:35.626 align:middle
let the Fed do our thing.

38:35.626 --> 38:36.076 align:middle
We're, you know,

38:36.076 --> 38:40.166 align:middle
we're not-we're-it's a-it's an
institution full of human beings

38:40.166 --> 38:42.536 align:middle
who work super hard
to get things right

38:42.536 --> 38:43.536 align:middle
for the benefit of the public.

38:43.536 --> 38:44.186 align:middle
We're all human.

38:44.186 --> 38:45.666 align:middle
Don't expect perfection.

38:46.036 --> 38:48.116 align:middle
But do expect us
to make, you know,

38:48.116 --> 38:50.306 align:middle
decisions without
political considerations

38:50.396 --> 38:53.026 align:middle
and the very best
analysis we can bring.

38:54.476 --> 38:55.926 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:55.926 --> 38:57.786 align:middle
Catarina. CATARINA SARAIVA.

38:58.716 --> 39:01.536 align:middle
Catarina Saraiva,
Bloomberg News.

39:01.946 --> 39:04.726 align:middle
How do you characterize
what you've heard

39:04.726 --> 39:07.196 align:middle
from your colleagues on
your decision to stay?

39:07.436 --> 39:08.996 align:middle
Do you have their support?

39:09.456 --> 39:12.846 align:middle
And then have you heard
concern from your colleagues

39:13.286 --> 39:17.316 align:middle
about continued legal attacks
from the executive branch?

39:17.316 --> 39:19.966 align:middle
Is this something
that others have,

39:19.966 --> 39:20.906 align:middle
you know, talked to you about?

39:21.466 --> 39:21.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:21.966 --> 39:26.356 align:middle
So I think that-I
don't want to report

39:26.356 --> 39:27.296 align:middle
on what my colleagues think.

39:27.296 --> 39:28.696 align:middle
They can-they can
speak for themselves.

39:28.726 --> 39:29.956 align:middle
But, you know, yeah,

39:29.956 --> 39:31.886 align:middle
there are-there are
widespread concerns

39:31.886 --> 39:33.346 align:middle
that these things may continue.

39:33.346 --> 39:34.896 align:middle
That's all-that's all I'll say.

39:35.466 --> 39:36.906 align:middle
And, you know, and that
would be a problem.

39:37.096 --> 39:39.876 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

39:40.316 --> 39:43.746 align:middle
And then I just also
wanted to ask

39:43.746 --> 39:46.876 align:middle
about Governor Waller's
speech on the Reserve Banks.

39:47.806 --> 39:51.586 align:middle
What, you know-do you have any
thoughts on centralizing some

39:51.586 --> 39:54.016 align:middle
of those functions in
the way he described?

39:54.516 --> 39:58.256 align:middle
And then have-you know, do-are
you concerned that something

39:58.256 --> 40:03.176 align:middle
like that could potentially be
a slippery slope to, you know,

40:03.176 --> 40:05.946 align:middle
to consolidate Reserve
Bank functions even more

40:06.266 --> 40:08.116 align:middle
in such a way where
you-you know,

40:08.116 --> 40:10.496 align:middle
the central bank
ultimately loses some

40:10.496 --> 40:12.586 align:middle
of that important
regional information?

40:12.586 --> 40:13.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:13.606 --> 40:16.956 align:middle
So we try to be good stewards

40:17.096 --> 40:19.536 align:middle
of the public's money,
and efficient.

40:19.786 --> 40:22.306 align:middle
And Chris, in particular-Chris
Waller is particularly

40:22.306 --> 40:23.176 align:middle
passionate about that.

40:23.676 --> 40:25.876 align:middle
Of course, so are the
Reserve-so are the presidents.

40:25.876 --> 40:29.616 align:middle
And, you know, it's a question
of how do you accomplish it.

40:30.156 --> 40:33.386 align:middle
We, of course-and Chris said
this in his speech-we, you know,

40:33.386 --> 40:36.756 align:middle
we want 12 strong
independent central banks

40:36.756 --> 40:39.256 align:middle
with their own staffs and
their own monetary policy views

40:39.256 --> 40:39.686 align:middle
and all that.

40:40.266 --> 40:42.946 align:middle
But, you know, there are
things that are done in all 12

40:42.946 --> 40:45.156 align:middle
which could well be done
at 1 much more efficiently

40:45.156 --> 40:46.126 align:middle
and with cost savings.

40:46.126 --> 40:48.786 align:middle
And so there's a
back-and-forth going on on that.

40:48.786 --> 40:50.666 align:middle
But everybody, everybody's
on the same page.

40:51.006 --> 40:54.716 align:middle
The other thing he
touched on was the idea

40:54.716 --> 40:57.706 align:middle
of removing Reserve Bank
presidents from office

40:57.806 --> 41:00.806 align:middle
over different, different
views on monetary policy.

41:00.806 --> 41:03.086 align:middle
And I would-I would just
agree with him so strongly

41:03.746 --> 41:06.356 align:middle
that that would be the
beginning of the end of,

41:06.546 --> 41:10.496 align:middle
of the Fed's ability to, to make
monetary policy independently.

41:10.496 --> 41:12.426 align:middle
If every Administration
could come in and do that,

41:13.016 --> 41:15.196 align:middle
you're just another cabinet
agency at that point.

41:15.196 --> 41:17.726 align:middle
So that's not something
that I would support.

41:17.726 --> 41:18.636 align:middle
Chris said the same thing.

41:18.766 --> 41:20.476 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:20.476 --> 41:20.786 align:middle
Christine.

41:21.546 --> 41:23.146 align:middle
CHRISTINE ROMANS.

41:23.826 --> 41:24.426 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

41:24.426 --> 41:26.006 align:middle
Christine Romans, NBC News.

41:26.006 --> 41:27.426 align:middle
I want to ask about legacy.

41:27.866 --> 41:29.676 align:middle
When the history
books are written,

41:29.836 --> 41:32.406 align:middle
how do you think
your stewardship

41:32.806 --> 41:35.546 align:middle
at the Fed will be remembered
for the past eight years?

41:35.896 --> 41:36.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:36.666 --> 41:39.656 align:middle
You know, I'm going to just
say that that's for-that's

41:39.656 --> 41:40.656 align:middle
for someone else to say.

41:40.776 --> 41:41.996 align:middle
I'll give you a mulligan
on that.

41:41.996 --> 41:42.476 align:middle
CHRISTINE ROMANS.

41:43.056 --> 41:43.326 align:middle
All right.

41:43.326 --> 41:47.016 align:middle
I'm going to ask you about
misbehaving inflation then.

41:47.296 --> 41:50.066 align:middle
You talked about those four big
shocks-supply shocks-over the

41:50.066 --> 41:53.196 align:middle
past five years and
inflation still misbehaving.

41:53.196 --> 41:54.976 align:middle
What's your message to
American families who feel

41:54.976 --> 41:57.116 align:middle
like inflation has
not been under control

41:57.116 --> 41:59.956 align:middle
for them really since,
since the COVID reopening?

42:00.376 --> 42:00.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:00.616 --> 42:03.366 align:middle
You know, we're committed
to bring inflation back

42:03.366 --> 42:06.926 align:middle
down to 2 percent
and sustainably.

42:07.326 --> 42:08.386 align:middle
That's our goal.

42:08.386 --> 42:10.666 align:middle
And we will-we'll stick
at it until that happens.

42:11.066 --> 42:12.966 align:middle
We keep getting-these
events keep happening

42:12.966 --> 42:14.516 align:middle
which keep driving up costs.

42:15.206 --> 42:19.826 align:middle
And, you know, the best thing
we can do is to use our tools

42:19.826 --> 42:22.116 align:middle
to guide inflation
back down to 2 percent.

42:22.686 --> 42:25.706 align:middle
I think trying to get there
really quickly could be very

42:25.706 --> 42:29.526 align:middle
costly in terms of job
loss and things like that.

42:29.586 --> 42:32.336 align:middle
But we try to get there
over time in a way

42:32.336 --> 42:33.916 align:middle
that does the least
damage possible.

42:33.916 --> 42:34.926 align:middle
And, you know, our commitment

42:34.926 --> 42:37.386 align:middle
to that is never-ending
and unshakable.

42:37.386 --> 42:37.776 align:middle
CHRISTINE ROMANS.

42:37.816 --> 42:40.186 align:middle
How would you describe
the economy outside

42:40.186 --> 42:41.776 align:middle
of the misbehaving inflation?

42:41.906 --> 42:45.546 align:middle
I mean, it's still
awfully resilient,

42:45.546 --> 42:46.876 align:middle
given all of the blows.

42:46.946 --> 42:48.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:48.066 --> 42:48.696 align:middle
I don't know that you can be

42:48.696 --> 42:51.456 align:middle
"awfully" resilient-so it's
actually quite resilient,

42:51.456 --> 42:52.766 align:middle
I would say, because
it's a positive thing,

42:52.766 --> 42:54.536 align:middle
if I can-if I can
have that amendment.

42:54.926 --> 42:59.306 align:middle
Yeah, you know, growth is
really solid across our economy.

42:59.716 --> 43:03.056 align:middle
Some of that is that
consumer spending is hanging

43:03.056 --> 43:03.696 align:middle
in pretty well.

43:03.966 --> 43:05.606 align:middle
The most recent data are good.

43:06.106 --> 43:09.206 align:middle
And some of it is just the
apparently insatiable demand

43:09.206 --> 43:11.036 align:middle
for data centers all
over the United States.

43:11.036 --> 43:14.146 align:middle
So a lot of business
investment going

43:14.146 --> 43:15.806 align:middle
into building data
centers, and every reason

43:15.806 --> 43:16.896 align:middle
to think that that continues.

43:17.246 --> 43:20.596 align:middle
So you've got an economy that's
growing at 2 percent or better.

43:20.756 --> 43:23.886 align:middle
PDFP, which is private
domestic final purchases,

43:24.506 --> 43:28.416 align:middle
which is really a better signal
of, of momentum in the economy,

43:28.416 --> 43:29.436 align:middle
is actually higher than that.

43:29.436 --> 43:32.666 align:middle
So that's, you know,
that's a positive thing.

43:32.786 --> 43:34.896 align:middle
If you look at the
unemployment rate,

43:34.896 --> 43:37.496 align:middle
it's 4.3 [percent],
so that's a low rate.

43:37.496 --> 43:38.346 align:middle
That's pretty close

43:38.346 --> 43:40.376 align:middle
to mainstream estimates
of the natural rate.

43:40.376 --> 43:41.566 align:middle
We've been there
for a long time.

43:42.266 --> 43:45.276 align:middle
So it's-it doesn't feel like
a good labor market to some

43:45.376 --> 43:48.786 align:middle
who don't have jobs because
quits are really low,

43:48.786 --> 43:49.976 align:middle
hires are really low,

43:50.366 --> 43:53.826 align:middle
and there's effectively
no new net job creation.

43:53.826 --> 43:58.026 align:middle
So that's, that's-you know, in
a sense, the labor market is

43:58.086 --> 44:02.056 align:middle
in balance, but it's an
unusual and uncomfortable kind

44:02.056 --> 44:03.636 align:middle
of a balance where people

44:03.636 --> 44:05.536 align:middle
who don't have jobs will
have a hard time breaking

44:05.536 --> 44:07.036 align:middle
in unless somebody
quits their job.

44:07.676 --> 44:09.606 align:middle
So that's pretty good.

44:09.606 --> 44:11.536 align:middle
You know, inflation is the
thing we need to work on.

44:12.106 --> 44:14.966 align:middle
And it's partly tariffs,
which we think-we think

44:14.966 --> 44:17.216 align:middle
that that inflation should
subside over the course

44:17.216 --> 44:20.176 align:middle
of this year because it's
kind of a one-time increase,

44:20.176 --> 44:21.056 align:middle
it shouldn't be repeated.

44:21.196 --> 44:22.906 align:middle
And that should start
happening pretty soon.

44:23.436 --> 44:26.396 align:middle
The energy inflation that
we're getting should go

44:26.396 --> 44:28.276 align:middle
through fairly quickly.

44:28.816 --> 44:31.166 align:middle
And we'll just have to
see how that works out.

44:31.166 --> 44:33.656 align:middle
In the meantime, you know,
we think our policy stance is

44:33.656 --> 44:36.046 align:middle
in a good place for us to
hold and wait developments.

44:37.176 --> 44:38.086 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:38.086 --> 44:40.086 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

44:40.846 --> 44:41.596 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

44:41.596 --> 44:43.156 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

44:43.466 --> 44:45.626 align:middle
At the risk of beating
the dead horse here,

44:45.626 --> 44:49.006 align:middle
clearly three members
objected to keeping

44:49.006 --> 44:50.646 align:middle
that easing bias
in the statement.

44:50.956 --> 44:55.486 align:middle
And you said that the majority
still didn't need-still didn't

44:55.486 --> 44:58.486 align:middle
need to move to new
language at this point.

44:58.836 --> 45:00.166 align:middle
So does the majority

45:00.166 --> 45:04.866 align:middle
of the Committee still have a
bias towards cuts at this point?

45:04.866 --> 45:07.996 align:middle
Or has the bias on the
Committee shifted away

45:07.996 --> 45:10.826 align:middle
from cuts towards holding or
hikes, if that was needed?

45:10.826 --> 45:11.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:11.456 --> 45:13.026 align:middle
So I think that, you know,

45:13.026 --> 45:17.696 align:middle
the center is moving
toward a more neutral place.

45:18.156 --> 45:20.316 align:middle
And that's sort of what
markets are saying, too.

45:21.056 --> 45:25.136 align:middle
I just think, you know,
there's a lot of signaling going

45:25.136 --> 45:26.926 align:middle
on when you change
guidance like that.

45:27.096 --> 45:31.996 align:middle
And so we just-I guess a
majority of us didn't feel

45:31.996 --> 45:35.216 align:middle
like we needed to send a
signal on that right now.

45:35.556 --> 45:37.456 align:middle
And-but maybe it'll
come to that.

45:37.566 --> 45:40.156 align:middle
And the reason is because, you
know, we're kind of waiting

45:40.156 --> 45:43.506 align:middle
to see what happens with,
with events in the Middle East

45:43.506 --> 45:44.466 align:middle
and what are the implications

45:44.466 --> 45:46.956 align:middle
of those events for
the U.S. economy.

45:47.126 --> 45:49.436 align:middle
So there was just
a-there's a group who feels

45:49.436 --> 45:51.066 align:middle
like we don't need to be
in a hurry to do that.

45:51.276 --> 45:52.026 align:middle
We get it.

45:52.346 --> 45:55.776 align:middle
And, of course, we will move
to a hiking bias if we want

45:55.776 --> 45:58.306 align:middle
to hike, and we'll move to
a neutral bias before that.

45:58.306 --> 46:00.246 align:middle
But there was a difference
over whether to do it

46:00.246 --> 46:04.196 align:middle
at this meeting-at a meeting at
which all but one of us agreed

46:04.366 --> 46:06.286 align:middle
that the-that the rate
decision was correct,

46:06.286 --> 46:07.106 align:middle
which was not to move.

46:07.106 --> 46:07.956 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

46:07.956 --> 46:09.436 align:middle
And you just said moments ago

46:09.436 --> 46:12.496 align:middle
that you believe Fed
independence is at risk.

46:12.906 --> 46:15.986 align:middle
Is it safe to say that you
want to stay on as a Governor

46:15.986 --> 46:17.866 align:middle
to serve as a check
and balance on that?

46:18.096 --> 46:20.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:20.146 --> 46:21.906 align:middle
I want to stay until
I-I will stay

46:21.906 --> 46:24.146 align:middle
until it's-I feel it's
appropriate for me to leave.

46:24.346 --> 46:27.016 align:middle
And, yes, that is-that is
really what is driving this.

46:28.076 --> 46:32.486 align:middle
I'm not-I'm not looking
to be, you know,

46:32.486 --> 46:35.616 align:middle
a high-profile dissident
or anything like that.

46:35.826 --> 46:40.006 align:middle
I'm more looking at the other
aspects of this and wanting

46:40.006 --> 46:43.266 align:middle
to see that things have
calmed down and are returning

46:43.266 --> 46:47.516 align:middle
to a traditional model of
working with the people

46:47.516 --> 46:49.706 align:middle
that you have and
bringing them to consensus

46:49.776 --> 46:51.766 align:middle
and respecting that consensus.

46:52.396 --> 46:54.386 align:middle
That's what-that's
what I'm hoping to see.

46:55.256 --> 46:57.506 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:57.736 --> 46:58.226 align:middle
Matt Egan.

47:00.356 --> 47:00.876 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

47:00.966 --> 47:01.616 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

47:01.616 --> 47:03.106 align:middle
Matt Egan with CNN.

47:03.186 --> 47:06.746 align:middle
You've made many tough decisions
in your time at the Fed.

47:07.366 --> 47:10.876 align:middle
And as your time as Chair
comes to a close and you think

47:10.876 --> 47:13.076 align:middle
about your tenure and
perhaps your legacy,

47:13.566 --> 47:15.976 align:middle
are there any decisions
that stand out as ones

47:15.976 --> 47:17.736 align:middle
that you're particularly
proud of?

47:17.886 --> 47:20.996 align:middle
And are there any that, with
the benefit of hindsight,

47:21.086 --> 47:22.266 align:middle
you would take a mulligan on?

47:23.596 --> 47:24.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:24.056 --> 47:25.526 align:middle
Yeah, it's hard.

47:25.526 --> 47:29.006 align:middle
I wouldn't want to single out
individual things at this point.

47:29.006 --> 47:31.416 align:middle
You know, I'll just
say, you know,

47:32.326 --> 47:35.736 align:middle
we-all of us together
have consistently tried

47:35.736 --> 47:38.356 align:middle
to do what we think is best
for the American people based

47:38.356 --> 47:42.026 align:middle
on our tools and our objectives
that Congress has given us.

47:42.666 --> 47:44.856 align:middle
It's been very challenging
because we've been

47:44.856 --> 47:48.226 align:middle
in a situation of supply
shock-supply shocks,

47:48.226 --> 47:50.006 align:middle
really, for six years.

47:50.566 --> 47:54.206 align:middle
And that's just a very different
situation than-for a very long

47:54.206 --> 47:57.936 align:middle
time, what the Fed and other
central banks were doing all the

47:57.936 --> 47:59.516 align:middle
time was demand management.

48:00.116 --> 48:03.276 align:middle
And, you know, and there was
always the inflation mandate,

48:03.276 --> 48:05.056 align:middle
but inflation was
low for 25 years.

48:05.056 --> 48:08.016 align:middle
So this is a very
different world

48:08.016 --> 48:09.766 align:middle
and a much more challenging
one where you have

48:09.766 --> 48:11.206 align:middle
to balance the two objectives.

48:11.206 --> 48:12.586 align:middle
And, by the way, central banks

48:12.586 --> 48:14.746 align:middle
that have an inflation
mandate have

48:14.746 --> 48:15.906 align:middle
to do exactly the same thing

48:15.906 --> 48:17.706 align:middle
because they're balancing
economic activity.

48:18.386 --> 48:19.676 align:middle
So that's been challenging.

48:19.846 --> 48:21.986 align:middle
And we've, you know, we've
tried to do our very best

48:21.986 --> 48:24.976 align:middle
through these really
challenging times.

48:24.976 --> 48:28.686 align:middle
And I'm really proud of
the work that I've done,

48:28.686 --> 48:29.776 align:middle
that my-that my colleagues

48:29.776 --> 48:31.956 align:middle
and I have done during
these, these years.

48:32.296 --> 48:32.966 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

48:32.996 --> 48:34.796 align:middle
Now, to follow up on
some of the discussion

48:34.796 --> 48:37.206 align:middle
around Fed independence,
can you explain

48:37.206 --> 48:40.896 align:middle
to the public why this
notion of Fed independence,

48:40.896 --> 48:44.216 align:middle
which might sound kind of
wonky to some, is so critical?

48:44.216 --> 48:45.466 align:middle
I mean, what are
the consequences

48:45.536 --> 48:48.946 align:middle
if either the Supreme Court
rules against Lisa Cook

48:48.946 --> 48:53.026 align:middle
or the Fed in the future
decides to make decisions more

48:53.026 --> 48:55.056 align:middle
around the political calendar
instead of the economic data?

48:55.056 --> 48:55.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:56.166 --> 48:59.186 align:middle
So every major advanced economy

48:59.186 --> 49:02.316 align:middle
in the world has made the same
decision the United States

49:02.316 --> 49:02.726 align:middle
has made.

49:03.516 --> 49:06.496 align:middle
And that is that they
want to take the making

49:06.496 --> 49:08.816 align:middle
of monetary policy, the
setting of interest rates

49:08.896 --> 49:11.216 align:middle
to support the economy, to
achieve maximum employment

49:11.216 --> 49:13.946 align:middle
and price stability-they
want to take that out

49:13.946 --> 49:17.626 align:middle
of the direct control
of elected politicians.

49:17.626 --> 49:20.266 align:middle
And the reason is elected
politicians are always running

49:20.266 --> 49:22.906 align:middle
for election and they'll
always want low rates,

49:22.906 --> 49:25.256 align:middle
and that will lead to
inflation over time.

49:25.636 --> 49:28.756 align:middle
So after, you know, literally
centuries of experience

49:28.756 --> 49:31.466 align:middle
with that, the whole world
moved to the different model.

49:31.606 --> 49:32.766 align:middle
And it's worked great.

49:32.766 --> 49:35.436 align:middle
I mean, this is-this is the
era in which inflation was

49:35.886 --> 49:37.236 align:middle
under control for 40 years.

49:37.236 --> 49:39.296 align:middle
Then we had the
pandemic-inflation everywhere

49:39.296 --> 49:40.036 align:middle
in the world.

49:40.036 --> 49:41.596 align:middle
And now we have inflation

49:41.596 --> 49:43.216 align:middle
that had gone pretty
much all the way back

49:43.216 --> 49:45.466 align:middle
to target-really close to
all the way back to target

49:45.796 --> 49:48.916 align:middle
and now is being buffeted by
the energy shock in the U.S.,

49:48.966 --> 49:50.836 align:middle
buffeted by the-by the
tariff shock as well.

49:51.356 --> 49:53.006 align:middle
But what I would say

49:53.006 --> 49:55.596 align:middle
to the general public
is-that's the backstory-is

49:55.596 --> 50:00.216 align:middle
that don't think about an
institution being independent.

50:00.216 --> 50:02.786 align:middle
Think about it this
way-that you want people

50:02.786 --> 50:04.986 align:middle
to make monetary policy
and set interest rates

50:05.336 --> 50:07.796 align:middle
to benefit the general
public and to try

50:07.796 --> 50:10.516 align:middle
to achieve economic goals,
which are maximum employment

50:10.576 --> 50:12.696 align:middle
and price stability,
and focus only on that

50:12.976 --> 50:15.636 align:middle
and ignore political
considerations,

50:15.636 --> 50:17.186 align:middle
completely ignore them.

50:17.186 --> 50:19.866 align:middle
It's-this isn't bipartisan,
this is nonpartisan.

50:20.276 --> 50:22.946 align:middle
So we want to just-we
just work directly

50:22.946 --> 50:24.636 align:middle
for the American people
doing these things.

50:25.036 --> 50:27.236 align:middle
We don't think, "Oh,
this-I want to do this

50:27.236 --> 50:29.436 align:middle
because the President
says it's a good idea

50:29.436 --> 50:32.126 align:middle
or because there's an election
coming up and I want to-I want

50:32.126 --> 50:33.886 align:middle
to speed up or slow
down the economy."

50:33.926 --> 50:35.666 align:middle
I mean, that-think about that.

50:35.666 --> 50:37.886 align:middle
If that's-if that-if
that's what we were doing,

50:38.646 --> 50:41.206 align:middle
we'd have no credibility,
markets would lose faith in us

50:41.606 --> 50:43.546 align:middle
and our ability to
control inflation

50:44.176 --> 50:47.296 align:middle
and have any respect would
be, you know, would be gone.

50:47.296 --> 50:50.576 align:middle
And let me say-whatever people
say, the markets believe

50:50.766 --> 50:52.916 align:middle
that we will produce
2 percent inflation.

50:52.916 --> 50:54.836 align:middle
If you look at longer-run
expectations,

50:55.216 --> 50:59.606 align:middle
markets believe that-there's no
sense in which our credibility

50:59.606 --> 51:00.736 align:middle
in the markets has weakened.

51:01.346 --> 51:02.306 align:middle
It's just not the case.

51:02.306 --> 51:05.286 align:middle
It's-people do get it that
this is our commitment

51:05.286 --> 51:07.276 align:middle
and that we will achieve
it and it's priced in.

51:07.646 --> 51:09.616 align:middle
If you disagree with that,
then you can go ahead

51:09.616 --> 51:10.996 align:middle
and bet against the markets.

51:11.046 --> 51:13.786 align:middle
But the markets are
pricing in Fed credibility.

51:13.786 --> 51:14.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:14.826 --> 51:17.286 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to
Richard for the last one.

51:17.286 --> 51:18.346 align:middle
RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

51:18.936 --> 51:21.436 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

51:21.436 --> 51:22.616 align:middle
Richard Escobedo with CBS News.

51:22.616 --> 51:25.526 align:middle
We've talked a lot
about gasoline prices,

51:25.526 --> 51:28.476 align:middle
and even you mentioned airline
ticket prices, both of which are

51:28.476 --> 51:30.466 align:middle
up dramatically because
of the war in Iran.

51:30.896 --> 51:33.216 align:middle
And so I wonder, are
you seeing that weigh

51:33.216 --> 51:35.976 align:middle
down consumer spending in
other parts of the economy?

51:36.326 --> 51:38.556 align:middle
And, if so, how worried are you

51:38.556 --> 51:39.956 align:middle
that that will be
a drag on growth?

51:40.266 --> 51:40.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:40.506 --> 51:42.366 align:middle
You don't see-you don't
see it in spending yet.

51:42.366 --> 51:43.046 align:middle
You really don't.

51:43.046 --> 51:45.416 align:middle
I mean, as one of
your colleagues said,

51:45.526 --> 51:49.116 align:middle
the economy has been
resilient-it really has-not just

51:49.366 --> 51:51.686 align:middle
this time, but it's been
remarkably resilient

51:51.686 --> 51:52.506 align:middle
for some years now.

51:52.506 --> 51:54.536 align:middle
The U.S. economy
has just powered

51:54.626 --> 51:55.756 align:middle
through shock after shock.

51:56.146 --> 51:57.466 align:middle
And consumers are
still spending.

51:57.466 --> 51:59.156 align:middle
And that's what-that's what
the banks will tell you,

51:59.446 --> 52:00.776 align:middle
credit card companies
will tell you.

52:00.776 --> 52:03.976 align:middle
The retail sales numbers that
we got most recently-people are

52:03.976 --> 52:04.706 align:middle
still spending.

52:04.896 --> 52:07.536 align:middle
And, you know, how
long can that go

52:07.536 --> 52:09.816 align:middle
on in a world where-if
gas prices were to go

52:09.816 --> 52:13.586 align:middle
up a bunch more, that's taking
otherwise spendable money

52:13.586 --> 52:14.486 align:middle
out of people's pockets.

52:14.486 --> 52:18.116 align:middle
But, right now, we don't
actually see much slowdown

52:18.436 --> 52:21.046 align:middle
in-yet from-certainly,
none from this.

52:21.046 --> 52:22.766 align:middle
But you think logically you will

52:22.766 --> 52:25.006 align:middle
because people have a certain
amount of money they spend.

52:25.006 --> 52:27.736 align:middle
If they're spending 25 percent
more on gas or something

52:27.736 --> 52:29.806 align:middle
like that, then, you
know, that's going to come

52:29.806 --> 52:30.646 align:middle
out of other spending.

52:30.646 --> 52:31.776 align:middle
But we-again, we
don't see it yet.

52:32.166 --> 52:32.696 align:middle
RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

52:32.856 --> 52:33.776 align:middle
One last thing.

52:33.906 --> 52:35.706 align:middle
You mentioned those
economies in Southeast Asia

52:35.706 --> 52:37.876 align:middle
that are particularly
dependent on petroleum.

52:38.466 --> 52:41.046 align:middle
They make a lot of the stuff
that American consumers buy.

52:41.046 --> 52:45.126 align:middle
So was there any discussion
today about whether

52:45.126 --> 52:47.616 align:middle
or not those costs
getting passed along

52:47.616 --> 52:51.146 align:middle
to consumers is a real
concern, and whether or not

52:51.146 --> 52:52.216 align:middle
that might push up inflation?

52:52.216 --> 52:52.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:52.476 --> 52:58.936 align:middle
So all of those things are
in-are in the models that we use

52:58.936 --> 53:00.056 align:middle
to calculate inflation.

53:00.436 --> 53:02.426 align:middle
So we-you know, they're
just parts.

53:02.936 --> 53:04.526 align:middle
You can ask about
anything like that,

53:04.526 --> 53:06.976 align:middle
and they are-they have a
place-the staff has a place

53:06.976 --> 53:10.316 align:middle
where they're looking at that
and pricing in what will happen

53:10.316 --> 53:12.716 align:middle
with higher prices and
that kind of thing.

53:12.716 --> 53:13.576 align:middle
So it's there.

53:13.816 --> 53:15.456 align:middle
The effects are not
that big yet.

53:15.796 --> 53:17.456 align:middle
You know, we're a huge economy.

53:17.886 --> 53:21.406 align:middle
The import sector is only
10 percent of the economy.

53:21.406 --> 53:23.906 align:middle
So we're not like a European
country where 50 percent

53:23.906 --> 53:27.756 align:middle
of the external-of GDP is
in the external sector.

53:28.196 --> 53:29.806 align:middle
We're also, you know,
as I mentioned,

53:29.806 --> 53:33.056 align:middle
we're an oil exporter, so
we're not feeling the same kind

53:33.056 --> 53:35.226 align:middle
of pain-and we're not
likely to feel the same kind

53:35.226 --> 53:38.806 align:middle
of pain-that economies
in Western Europe and,

53:38.806 --> 53:39.946 align:middle
certainly, in Asia are feeling.

53:40.596 --> 53:42.066 align:middle
Anyway, thank you
very much, everyone.

53:43.326 --> 53:47.856 align:middle
I won't see you next time.

53:48.356 --> 53:50.610 align:middle
[Laughter]

